12704000000,,\\r\\nrelated_ar_markdown_50,p_a787b0506129692c9599628a,False,2027-03-01 operating_cash_cost,0.2500000000000000000000000000000000,2.000000000000000000000000000000000,14.99999380875,883587,21.12,-39.51816356643318959906465026196510,6.15112704000000,,\\r\\nterminal_k_13,p_fc76a05e76f173b4b7020a8b,True,,0.2500000000000000000000000000000000,2.000000000000000000000000000000000,2.45277952875,144483,21.12,40.61492825778587139172882762147335,6.15112704000000,442.0358305265183472172903381477447,0.136361039486990116381701917455241\\r\\nterminal_k_19,p_88de423631ab0ead3eb6d63c,True,,0.2500000000000000000000000000000000,2.000000000000000000000000000000000,2.45277952875,144483,21.12,40.61492825778587139172882762147335,6.15112704000000,291.5787853593900424643820892651450,0.045625160838192506986155726990147\\r\\nterminal_g_zero,p_e626969bd137f377fc375304,True,,0.2500000000000000000000000000000000,2.000000000000000000000000000000000,2.45277952875,144483,21.12,40.61492825778587139172882762147335,5.971968000000,347.0094364822974966512383901542449,0.082662507901092769358705260868229\\r\\nterminal_roic_10,p_0a41f6b2a935e5a9e2415f5b,True,,0.2500000000000000000000000000000000,2.000000000000000000000000000000000,2.45277952875,144483,21.12,40.61492825778587139172882762147335,6.15112704000000,310.6580041965671428842698754566235,0.058964375202188681523391245726338\\r\\n\"","\"# Rockingdeals Circular Economy Limited\\n## 1. Executive Verdict\\n\\n**Research incomplete / evidence Hold. BQS NS · GQS NS · IAS NS. Not a compounder designation or an investable ranking.** The reported growth is real as a disclosure, but its organic economics, cash realization and consolidated profit reliability are not established sufficiently to underwrite a five-year return. The observed full-paid share close is ₹226.35 on **16 October 2026**, not a verified executable 19 October price.\\n\\nThree findings dominate. First, the FY26 consolidated auditor relied on management-supplied, unaudited Dubai accounts contributing ₹4.0059 crore, about 40% of reported group PAT. The opinion remains unmodified; this is **not** an adverse opinion or a fraud finding. Second, FY25 stock worth ₹8.58 crore was capitalized into fixed assets, while reported cash-flow capex presents a much larger gross outflow than the economic cash movement can safely establish. Third, group FY26 CFO was approximately **negative ₹21.43 crore**, with ₹50.03 crore inventory and ₹19.71 crore receivables. These matters outweigh a superficially modest reported P/E. [E02–E07; FY26 annual report pp127–128,139,151–153; FY25 pp73–74.](https://archives.nseindia.com/annual_reports/SME_AR_31795_ROCKINGDCE_2025_2026_A_9082837_07092026205811.pdf)\\n\\nA future ₹21.12 crore rights call is neither current cash nor a new-share allotment. Existing partly-paid shares already bring total issued claims to 8,299,000, against 5,659,000 currently fully-paid shares. Funding, minority treatment and valuation must preserve both classes. No purchase, sale or allocation is proposed. The useful next step is reconciliation, not a price target.\\n\\n## 2. Research Header, Gates and Data Confidence\\n\\n- Exact issuer: Rockingdeals Circular Economy Limited, formerly Technix Electronics Limited/Private Limited. NSE Emerge **ROCKINGDCE**, ISIN **INE0PTR01012**, series SM; separate partly-paid **ROCKPP**, ISIN **IN90PTR01010**, series E1.\\n- Information cutoff: **20 October 2026, Asia/Kolkata**. Market window ends **19 October 2026**. Later information is not used to judge prior promises.\\n- Framework: supplied **v3.0, updated 3 October 2026**; exact source hash in framework_provenance.json. Deep research with unresolved evidence, not a completed favorable recommendation.\\n- Scope: consolidated latest accounts; original standalone and RHP-restated history kept separate. Indian Accounting Standards under the Companies Act, **not assumed Ind AS**. Currency ₹ crore except explicit per-share/lot amounts; RHP historical source units are hundreds of rupees.\\n- Horizon: five years from 19 October 2026. Nominal equity hurdle **15%**, sensitivities 12% and 18%, before investor taxes. Terminal operating discount rate 16%, independent of the equity hurdle; 13% and 19% sensitivities. Reference liquidity position ₹5 lakh is an illustration, not a suggested investment.\\n\\n| Gate | Status | Reason |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| G1 | Conditional pass / monitor | Unmodified opinions and no substantiated integrity failure; material transparency/related-entity concerns remain. |\\n| G2 | HOLD | Material unauditedDubaiPAT; original/restatedvintages; restrictedcash/guarantee/segment/cashcapex conflicts. No favorableinvestmentconclusion. |\\n| G3 | HOLD | Currentfunding and usablecash cannot be verified; model failures are conditional, not evidence of actual default. |\\n| G4 | HOLD | Observed5%participation cannot fit1lot; incomplete60sessionrowcoverage and unverifieddepth/restrictions. |\\n\\nConfidence is **Low overall**, set by the weakest material area: usable cash, unaudited component earnings and comparable organic economics. Identity and retrieved price/lot facts have High retrieval confidence; ordinary historical headline values and governance assessment Medium; growth attribution, capital cohorts, current funding and valuation Low. A known arithmetic identity is not evidence that the underlying economic claim is reliable.\\n\\n## 3. Scorecard and Calculation Audit\\n\\nScores use the exact v3 factors. BQS weights: GOV20%, MOAT20%, REV12%, DUR10%, CAP12%, CASH10%, BAL8%, EXEC8%. GQS: REV40%, DUR20%, EARN15%, CAP15%, CASH10%. IAS: final unrounded BQS25%, R35%, D20%, **P price-implied expectations10%**, L10%. No factor is replaced by a probability estimate. Canonical factors are reused, never rescored for a different composite.\\n\\n| Composite | Final | Coverage | Known subtotal | Raw range | Cap | Effective range |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| BQS | NS | 76.00% | 2.4800 | 2.7200–4.8800 | None | 2.7200–4.8800 |\\n| GQS | NS | 30.00% | 1.0000 | 1.7000–8.0000 | 5 | 1.7000–5.0000 |\\n| IAS | NS | 20.00% | 0.6000 | 1.4000–8.6000 | None | 1.4000–8.6000 |\\n\\n| Factor | Score | Confidence | Evidence | Basis | Contrary / limitation |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| GOV | 4 | Medium | E02,E03,E08,E14 | Unmodified opinions and disclosure of unusual inventory capitalization are positives, but repeated presentation/cash/guarantee inconsistencies and material unaudited component limit minority-owner confidence. | No established diversion, fraud or adverse opinion; do not substitute concerns for a substantiated G1 failure. |\\n| MOAT | 3 | Medium | E13,E20,E16 | Procurement/disposal relationships and secondary sales network create practical access; no demonstrated exclusivity, switching costs or durable abnormal cash returns. | Brand contracts exist; sourcing knowledge can have local value despite absent quantified retention. |\\n| REV | NS | Low | E04,E17 | Nominal growth is strong but related-party, restatement, geography and new-perimeter shifts prevent verified5yorganic/3yfallback measure. | Standalone FY24–26 still grows sharply; uncertainty is attribution, not denial of reported sales. |\\n| DUR | 4 | Medium | E12,E13,E20 | Excess-stock/reuse demand is persistent, but profitable scale requires inventory funding and new geography/project execution. | Large addressable reverse-commerce flows and successful peers show possible runway. |\\n| CAP | NS | Low | E03,E11,E17 | No clean comparable organic incremental invested-capital/commissioning cohorts; inventory-to-PPE and inherited land debt break naive ROIC comparisons. | Closing ratios and operatingICproxies are disclosed as diagnostics rather than hidden. |\\n| CASH | 2 | Medium | E05,E06,E07 | Two large cashabsorbing years acrossFY24–26, receivables/inventory growth and weak funding-quality evidence persist despiteFY25positiveCFO. | FY25CFOpositive; some cash absorption can fund profitable future growth, but realization unverified. |\\n| BAL | 3 | Medium | E07,E08,E09 | Large working-capital exposure, uncertain availablecash, callableborrowings and externalguarantee weaken resilience. | Reported equity exceeds debt and auditors report no default; does not prove neartermcashavailability. |\\n| EXEC | 3 | Medium | E12,E14,E04 | Promised expansion/JV subsequently withdrawn and e-waste use remains small; disclosure corrections and CFOturnover add execution risk. | Revenue scale increased and Dubaisubsidiary became active; attribution and cashverification remain weak. |\\n| EARN | NS | Low | E02,E03,E17 | Dubai72.85%reportedPATmargin and40%groupPAT unaudited; historicalreclassification undermines clean earningsquality persistence. | Group opinion unmodified and standalonePATpositive; no unsupportedwriteoff of all earnings. |\\n| R | NS | Low | E07,E18 | No defensiblecurrentbaseIRR with unknownbridgefunding/reliability; failedpathsnotconvertedtoordinaryreturns. | Conditional improvedeconomicpaths can earnterminalvalue, without makingthem probable. |\\n| D | 3 | Medium | E06,E08,E19 | Agedrelateddebtor/inventorymarkdown, externalguarantee and impairedliquidity create permanentloss channels. | Assetbacking and uncalledrights provide potentialbuffers if collected/realized. |\\n| P | NS | Low | E02,E07,E18 | Price-implied expectations cannot be scored confidently: reverse operating-margin, sales and funding requirements depend on unresolved cash and group earnings, even though conditional thresholds are calculated. | Cash-linked reverse grids and terminal sensitivities show what the stale full-paid quote would require; unverified assumptions are not automatically cheapness. |\\n| L | NS | Low | E15,E19 | Observedconservative5%volume admitszero250sharelots; missing7sessions and no depth/circuits preventcompleteexecutionrating. | 53 primarysessionsandcurrentmasterallowboundedobservedliquiditydiagnostics; notpurelysecondaryND. |\\n\\nFor known weighted subtotal K and missing weight m, bounds are K+m to K+10m. Missing values remain NS, not zero, 5 or a reweighted average. Persistent CASH=2 caps GQS at 5; it does not make an incomplete GQS numerically scoreable. No substantiated G1 or G3 failure has been established, so no unsupported governance/solvency cap of 3 is applied. G2 Hold blocks a live IAS. Supplementary nested IAS bounds are calculated from BQS bounds in score_audit.json, not presented as a score. R would use supported base-case IRR less the15% equity hurdle and the exact v3 interval schedule; without a defensible supported base return it staysNS. Boundary tests are retained in score_audit.json. All computations use Decimal and presentation-only half-up rounding. No historical factor exceeds the Medium-confidence ceiling of 8.\\n\\n## 4. Business Overview\\n\\nRockingdeals buys and redistributes excess, open-box, refurbished, slow-moving or end-of-life consumer inventory. Its economic opportunity is acquiring stock below realizable selling value and finding secondary demand. The risk is **ownership of uncertain inventory**, not merely operating an asset-light marketplace. A logo or sourcing agreement does not establish exclusivity, recurring volumes, take-or-pay terms, manufacturer buyback, retail warranty coverage or a protected margin. [E13,E20; RHP business description and FY26 inventory/accounting policies.](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/emerge/corporates/content/RockingDeals_RHP.pdf)\\n\\nThe listed issuer must not be confused with similarly named **Rockingdeals Private Limited (RDPL)**, a related external entity and debtor/guarantee beneficiary. Harkishanji Products and AAA Services are also related counterparties, not automatically eliminated group subsidiaries. Current consolidated subsidiaries are Sustainquest Private Limited and Dubai Rocking Deals General Trading LLC, both wholly owned by the latest report. The remaining 42% of Sustainquest was acquired in March 2025; its disclosure says it had no business before acquisition. Earlier Digi2L joint-venture announcements do not establish an ongoing 58% economic stake after the March 2025 termination. No minority interest is assumed in the latest model. [E06,E08,E12; acquisition disclosure S45 and AR26 subsidiary/RPT notes.](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/rockingdeals_29032025160327_intimationofwos.pdf)\\n\\nThe business is expanding beyond its earlier liquidation trade into pre-owned products, Dubai distribution, e-waste and grocery retail. Each adds distinct procurement, collection, compliance or execution requirements. They are not interchangeable “circular economy” revenue streams and no uncommissioned e-waste capacity is granted revenue automatically.\\n\\n## 5. Moat / Industry Position\\n\\nSourcing relationships, knowledge of realizable prices and an outlet/distributor network may produce local access advantages. Demonstrated durable barriers are weaker: no independently verified exclusive supply, customer retention cohorts, stable category-level returns or cash-backed pricing premium. Stocks can become obsolete while accounting value remains unchanged; larger platforms and specialist refurbishers compete for both supply and demand. The lower-of-cost-and-NRV policy is a required valuation convention, not independent evidence of realizable stock value. [E13,E20; S13,S22,S26.]\\n\\n**Named primary economic peers, not an indiscriminate valuation basket:**\\n\\n| Peer | Primary observation | Economic lesson / comparability limit |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| GNG Electronics Limited | FY26 revenue ₹1,891.1 crore, EBITDA ₹200.5 crore, PAT ₹132 crore; 727,000 devices in its release | A much larger ICT refurbisher demonstrates scale and reported ~10.6% EBITDA/~7.0% PAT margins. Product, geography and processing mix differ; annual cash-flow retrieval was incomplete, so no CFO or current peer multiple is invented. [S49](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/GNGNSE_05052026194354_Press_Release_FY26_Financial_Results.pdf) |\\n| Newjaisa Technologies Limited | FY26 revenue ₹40.4882 crore versus ₹65.6551 crore; PAT −₹17.4189 crore versus −₹1.1318 crore | Weaker refurbishment comparator illustrates stock and demand risk. Auditor KAM inventory write-off ₹12.3810 crore differs from broader cash-flow balance-written-off ₹12.4026 crore. It is not an identical mixed-liquidation business. [S30](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/NEWJAISA2020_27052026195417_OutcomeBM_Financialsresults.pdf) |\\n| Liquidity Services, Inc. | FY25 to September: revenue US$476.669m, EBIT US$35.066m, PAT US$28.093m, CFO US$66.758m | Marketplace/consignment economics differ from owned inventory: revenue includes US$310.538m purchase sales and US$166.131m consignment fees. CFO includes US$14.490m stock compensation and US$5.241m inventory release; capex/software US$7.800m and acquisitions US$6.496m. GMV is not revenue, and cash conversion is not all owner cash. [S31](https://investors.liquidityservices.com/static-files/5bce319c-3b64-46e4-a77e-5560a376ab64) |\\n\\nThese disclose a stronger scale comparator, a weaker stock-risk comparator and a differentiated liquidation marketplace. Different currencies, fiscal calendars, acquisitions and gross/net accounting preclude unadjusted multiple transplantation. Their current market EV/P/E values are ND here; this is an economic comparison, not a fabricated peer valuation average.\\n\\n## 6. Revenue Growth & Scale\\n\\n**Originally issued pre-listing history, kept separate from restatements:**\\n\\n| FY | Original revenue₹cr | Original PAT₹cr | Original CFO₹cr | Cash₹cr | Source/scope |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| 2020 | 17.5819906 | 0.1214223 | 2.5847970 | 1.4719608 | Original standalone; FY20 report financial statements |\\n| 2021 | 10.5563213 | 0.0968567 | -1.7833665 | 0.1319961 | Original standalone; FY21 pp20–22 |\\n| 2022 | 14.8281641 | 0.1250846 | NS | 0.3011493 | Original standalone; FY22 pp23–24; no original CF statement |\\n| 2023 | 15.0100814 | 1.4503014 | 0.4188731 | 0.0645216 | Original standalone; FY23 pp8–10 |\\n\\n**Restated/comparative series used for labelled diagnostics:**\\n\\n| FY | Revenue ₹cr | PAT ₹cr | CFO ₹cr | Reported cashcapex ₹cr | Scope |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| 2021 | 10.5563 | -0.0148 | -1.4971 | NS | RHP restated standalone, hundred-rupee source normalized to crore |\\n| 2022 | 14.8282 | 0.1437 | 0.5621 | NS | RHP restated standalone |\\n| 2023 | 15.0101 | 1.5434 | 0.4189 | 0.0946 | RHP restated standalone, differs from later comparative |\\n| 2024 | 49.5612 | 5.2077 | -15.3381 | 1.7867 | Original standalone |\\n| 2025 | 55.4351 | 5.3377 | 2.9957 | 9.9975 | Consolidated comparative in FY26, group newly formed |\\n| 2026 | 119.8364 | 10.1309 | -21.4300 | 3.1200 | Consolidated FY26 annual report, cashflow rounded to lakh |\\n\\nThe table deliberately labels vintages. FY23 RHP PAT ₹1.5433914 crore differs from the later FY24 comparative ₹1.4503 crore; related staff expense differs by approximately ₹0.0931 crore. FY21 originally audited PAT and later comparisons also differ. FY22 originally issued accounts did not contain a cash-flow statement; the CFO shown is a later comparative/restatement. These are not one untouched six-year series. [E04,E17; original reports S06–S09, RHP S26, AR24 S04.]\\n\\n| FY | Revenue addition ₹cr | Reported growth | Organic attribution |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| 2022 | 4.2718 | 40.47% | NS |\\n| 2023 | 0.1819 | 1.23% | NS |\\n| 2024 | 34.5511 | 230.19% | NS |\\n| 2025 | 5.8739 | 11.85% | NS |\\n| 2026 | 64.4013 | 116.17% | NS |\\n\\n| Window | Nominal reported CAGR | Organic CAGR |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| 3 years | 99.86% | NS |\\n| 5 years | 62.56% | NS |\\n| 10 years | NS | NS |\\n\\nFY26 consolidated revenue addition was ₹64.4013 crore. Standalone addition was ₹45.3076 crore; the group-minus-parent revenue difference was ₹19.0937 crore, about 29.65% of group addition. That is a perimeter bridge, **not a claim that the balance was verified organic volume growth**. Group subsidiaries were newly formed/expanded, related-party channels changed, and comparable units, selling prices, category mix, return rates and gross-versus-net policies by stream are unavailable. Standalone FY26 revenue of ₹100.7427 crore is useful corroboration of scale, but not a complete organic decomposition. [E04; S02 p39.]\\n\\nPre-IPO related-party sales were a large share of revenue: approximately 50.19%, 72.90% and 52.99% in FY21–23. The FY24 RPT/customer channel is similarly significant. By FY26 disclosed related sales were much smaller, yet the RDPL receivable increased. Reducing a reported RPT sales percentage does not prove independent sell-through or collection of the legacy debtor. Customer concentration and geographical/category detail are not assumed away. Organic REV remains NS; neither a high nominal CAGR nor a pandemic base solves that attribution problem. [E06,E17; RHP RPT/customer concentration and AR26 RPT notes.]\\n\\n**Latest comparable half-year disclosure and reconciliation:**\\n\\n| Period | Revenue₹cr | PAT₹cr | Vintage/limitation |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| FY25H1 | 21.3635 | 3.1957 | DerivedFY25annual55.4351/5.3377 lessreportedH2revenue34.0716/PAT2.1420; notquarterlyCF |\\n| FY25H2 | 34.0716 | 2.1420 | ComparativeinJune24FY26results |\\n| FY26H1 | 55.0456 | 5.0391 | June24FY26results comparative |\\n| FY26H2 | 64.7909 | 4.8707 | ReportedH2June24FY26results; doesnot sumtoannualPAT owingtoDTA/vintagepresentation |\\n| FY26H2_annual_minus_H1 | 64.7908 | 5.0918 | LatestannualminusH1; derived, notanindependenthalfyearfiling |\\n\\nFY26 H2 reported PAT plus H1 does not equal the full-year figure: the June results leave₹0.2236 crore deferred-tax credit outside the half-year sum, while the later annual report changes full-year PAT by₹0.0025 crore. Latest annual minus H1 is explicitly a derived alternative, not substituted for the filed H2 figure. FY26 H2 reported PAT was below H1 despite higher sales. No H1FY27 results were available within the research cutoff, and annual cash flow is not allocated into invented quarters. [S32,S41,S02.]\\n\\n## 7. Growth Quality & Reinvestment Runway\\n\\nThere is an enduring economic need to clear surplus consumer stock and extend product life. Monetizing that demand profitably requires procurement discipline, saleable quality, warranties, efficient inventory turns, collection and funding. E-waste creates a different capital/regulatory process; grocery retail adds physical rollout and shrinkage. There is no verified installed-capacity utilization series or commissioned project return cohort that supports a capital-light extrapolation.\\n\\n| Years | ΔNOPAT | Δminimum IC | Same-window proxy | Lagged ΔIC | Lagged proxy | Organic scoreable |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| 3 | 8.2566 | 65.5758 | 12.59% | 37.2930 | 22.14% | No |\\n| 5 | 9.8062 | 67.8158 | 14.46% | NS | NS | No |\\n\\nNumerator is analytical operating NOPAT at a constant 25.17% tax rate, not reported PAT. The minimum operating-capital denominator is receivables + inventory + net PPE − trade payables. Financing capital is equity + debt − total reported cash; residuals expose omitted advances, taxes and accruals rather than forcing reconciliation. Both closing and average ratios are shown later. Three- and five-year incremental and lagged-capital diagnostics do **not** establish organic incremental ROIC: original/restated balance sheets, group formation, unaudited Dubai profits and stock-to-PPE conversion contaminate them. An original FY20 capital endpoint does exist: minimum operating IC is₹10.4104243 crore. There is no comparable RHP-restated FY20 endpoint, so that original balance is not spliced into the five-year lagged series and the lagged return remainsNS. [E03,E11,E17.]\\n\\nFY26 reported ROCE of 15.87% uses EBIT ₹13.7548 crore divided by closing capital ₹86.6740 crore, despite an average-capital caption. It includes other income and must not be silently called independently verified operating ROIC. The model consequently funds inventory, receivables, advances, maintenance and growth capex explicitly. Terminal reinvestment is at least g×NOPAT/15% assumed incremental ROIC, with a 10% sensitivity, as well as the structural working-capital/capex requirement. “Small size” and a large market are not substitutes for those economics.\\n\\n## 8. Management, Governance & Execution\\n\\n**Audits read, proportionate conclusions:** original FY20 and FY21 opinions are unmodified, with CARO reporting and IFC exemption. Original FY22 and FY23 opinions are unmodified, but CARO/IFC are not applicable under the stated small-company basis; they are not affirmative clean internal-controls audits. FY23's later public-company conversion does not retroactively change that scope. Original FY20/21 annexures contain stale boilerplate dates. FY24 audit/annexures, FY25 standalone and consolidated audit/IFC and FY26 equivalents were examined. AKAR & Associates was reappointed for the term beginning September 2024 through 2029. [E17; audit_evidence.json; S02–S09.]\\n\\nFY25's EOM specifically flags ₹8.58 crore inventory capitalization. Statutory arrears and external guarantee disclosures deserve attention, without being relabeled an adverse opinion. FY26 Dubai assets ₹4.5027 crore, revenue ₹5.4987 crore, PAT ₹4.0059 crore and net cash flows ₹1.9610 crore come from management-supplied unaudited information. The resulting 72.85% reported Dubai PAT margin and about 39.54% share of group PAT demand a component audit and a transaction/tax reconciliation, not an allegation. The July segment-response statement that no individual geography met quantitative criteria needs reconciliation with that material result; segment profit and PAT are not assumed identical. [E02,E03,E14; AR26 pp127–128,135–136; S47.]\\n\\nRPTs are material even where current sales are small. FY26 related purchases were ₹1.4046 crore; KMP remuneration ₹0.5729 crore. RDPL receivable ₹9.103 crore versus ₹8.3868 crore exceeds related FY26 sales by a wide margin. CARO identifies ₹8 crore guarantee for Sustainquest and ₹4.95 crore for external RDPL. The former supports consolidated exposure and must not simply be added again to group debt; the latter is a separate contingent stress. A consolidated note with nil component rows but total ₹4.97 crore does not override the ₹12.95 crore gross CARO disclosure. Obtain a counterparty-level roll-forward. [E06,E08; AR26 pp77,109,162–166.]\\n\\nPromoter capital changed with rights dilution. The FY26 capital note shows approximately 50.95% from Aman/Kulbir holdings, versus ~65.11% a year earlier. A declaration dated 7 April 2026 and exchange-filed 17 June says no encumbrance was created during FY26, but its annexure uses a different historical/full-paid-looking share denominator. That is not a verified current pledge-free float calculation. Full-class beneficial ownership and current encumbrance need reconciliation; no blanket “zero pledge” assertion is made. [S48; AR26 pp143–144.]\\n\\n**Dated promise versus outcome audit:**\\n\\n| Statement | Outcome by cutoff | Assessment |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| May 2024: Dubai operations Q2/Q3FY25, meaningful FY26; integration to reduce RPT | Dubai incorporated December 2024 and reports FY26 activity; component unaudited; RDPL debtor remains large | Partial operational delivery, no verified cash-quality resolution |\\n| November 2024: Digi2L JV, 20+ brands, 100 franchise stores in two years, an ambiguous EBITDA-floor response followed by an explicit PAT10% confidence claim | JV terminated March2025; no verified store cohort; franchise target’s two-year deadline is November2026, still future at cutoff | JV not delivered; do not falsely mark a not-yet-due store promise failed |\\n| December2024: ₹100cr rollout within12months, ₹400–500cr FY27 revenue, first e-waste phase in H1FY26 | Proposed ₹535 preferential equity/warrants withdrawn March2025; e-waste cumulative use only₹0.25cr by March2026 | Original financing/commissioning not demonstrated; FY27 target requires ~234–317% annual growth from FY26 |\\n| July2026: AED770,000 loan to Dubai at9% for one year | Approval disclosure says outstanding nil at approval; later disbursement not verified | Not cash already transferred; eliminate internally in consolidated model |\\n| October16: first grocery mart to open October20, investment₹0.50cr | Announcement located, independent commissioning proof not located at cutoff | Planned, not assumed delivered |\\n\\nSources: official calls S21–S22; expansion S28; cancellations S29,S35; monitor S27; loan S36; retail S01. The November2024 call also describes collections within roughly one month and no bad debts; FY26 ageing and RDPL balances require a bridge to that claim. CFO resignation and later MD/CFO signing, corrected machine-readable results and other filing errors are execution/transparency flags, not proof of dishonesty. Governance score4 is a cautious supported judgment; G1 has not been forced to fail.\\n\\n## 9. Financial Forensics\\n\\n| Metric | FY24 | FY25 | FY26 |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| operating_ebitda | 7.5185 | 10.3990 | 16.2899 |\\n| operating_ebit | 7.2526 | 7.8528 | 13.3516 |\\n| normalized_pat_proxy | 4.9878 | 5.2565 | 9.8272 |\\n| minimum_operating_ic | 32.0306 | 45.7693 | 76.7076 |\\n| financing_ic | 33.3458 | 47.2091 | 81.6149 |\\n| closing_roic_proxy | 0.1694 | 0.1284 | 0.1302 |\\n| average_ic_roic_proxy | 0.2515 | 0.1511 | 0.1631 |\\n| reported_fcf_proxy | -17.1248 | -7.0018 | -24.5500 |\\n| inventory | 22.9137 | 25.6845 | 50.0258 |\\n| ar | 5.8831 | 9.2796 | 19.7100 |\\n| ap | 2.2316 | 2.1115 | 6.1254 |\\n| debt | 0.0833 | 7.4883 | 15.3607 |\\n| cash | 1.7936 | 0.6730 | 5.0590 |\\n| end_dso | 43.3269 | 61.0994 | 60.0331 |\\n| end_inventory_days_operating_cost_proxy | 198.9287 | 208.1628 | 176.3403 |\\n| interest_cover | 23.7013 | 14.4672 | 12.4421 |\\n\\nOperating EBITDA removes all other income; operating EBIT excludes it as well. “Normalized PAT” in this table is only reported PAT less other income after analytical tax, not repaired economic earnings. Dubai uncertainty and inventory valuation cannot be cured with a made-up exceptional adjustment. Ratio rows are dimensionless; days are labelled. FY24–26 reported FCF proxies use reported cash-flow capex and therefore carry the noncash reconciliation warnings below. [E02–E07,E11; S02–S04,S41.]\\n\\n| Period / comparability | CFO sum | PAT sum | Ratio of sums |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| FY22_26_mixed_vintage_diagnostic | -32.7914 | 22.3634 | -146.63% |\\n| FY24_26_mixed_scope_diagnostic | -33.7724 | 20.6763 | -163.34% |\\n| FY25_26_consolidated | -18.4343 | 15.4686 | -119.17% |\\n\\nCFO/PAT is a ratio of **sums**, never an average of annual ratios. Mixed-vintage/mixed-scope windows are labelled diagnostics. FY25 CFO was positive, but FY24 and FY26 absorbed substantially more operating cash than profit generated. Capex is below CFO and does not explain a negative operating cash flow.\\n\\n**Cash bridge and capital classification:** FY26 annual-report rounded lakh bridge is PBT1,268 + depreciation294 + interest107 − interest income32 = pre-WC1,637. Inventory−2,434, receivables−1,043, long advances+37, other current assets−633, payables+401, other liabilities+125 and provisions+43 lead to reported cash generated−1,866, tax−277 and CFO−2,143. Rounded lines contain a ₹4 lakh reconciliation residual, retained explicitly. June24 results instead report CFO−₹21.4693 crore and PAT₹10.1334 crore versus annual-report PAT₹10.1309 crore. These are different disclosure vintages, not two independently confirming exact numbers. [S02 p139; S41.]\\n\\nFY25 reported fixed-asset cashflow of ₹9.9975 crore coexists with ₹8.58 crore stock-to-fixed-assets capitalization. Subtracting the latter yields ₹1.4175 crore cashcapex and ₹1.5782 crore FCF **only as a diagnostic reconciliation**, not an established corrected statement. The EOM alone does not establish whether all capitalized goods were opening stock or were bought and deployed during the year. An inventory roll-forward and operating-versus-investing cash classification bridge are required; no mechanical corrected CFO is asserted. FY26 land addition of ₹3.1111 crore includes assumed RIICO debt of ₹2.0124406 crore payable over three years, while cashflow shows about₹3.12 crore PPE purchase. Do not both treat that whole amount as cash spent and add inherited debt as a second purchase; the historical mismatch remains open. Forecast cashcapex and amortization are separately modelled, rather than extrapolating the unreconciled presentation. [E03,E11; S02 pp145,159; S03 pp73–74.]\\n\\n**Working-capital and funding risks:** inventory is roughly₹50 crore, with no SKU-level age, NRV cushion, warranty/return cohort or liquidation recovery history. Receivables include₹8.38 crore older than one year; management describes them as good. Supplier advances are₹6.50 crore versus₹1.23 crore. Overdue related debtor balances can create apparent demand and absorb funds without cash profit. Inventory days use operating cost as a proxy, not a claimed audited COGS-cycle disclosure. [E06,E20; S02 pp151–153.]\\n\\nReported cash₹5.059 crore includes cash-in-hand₹2.57 crore and short deposits₹1.58 crore. The monitoring report says unspent designated e-waste money₹3.99 crore is in an ICICI FD₹4.30 crore maturing7April2027. That FD exceeds the annual-report deposit line and cannot be added again. The model tests explicit usablecash allocations of₹0,₹1.069 and₹3.479 crore; none is asserted as the actual October balance. The₹3.479 usablecash branch pairs with only₹1.58 restricted openingcash, not₹3.99, and ring-fences₹7.23 of the future call; total opening cash never exceeds₹5.059. Monitor table rows total₹21.12 crore raised but one total line shows₹45.27 crore. The original offer proposed₹45.272 crore gross and₹0.80 crore fees, while actual allotment implies₹42.24 crore gross across both instalments. Monitor costs₹0.37 crore differ from annual cashflow issue expenses~₹0.33 crore. Residual fee and proportional use-of-proceeds reconciliation are unresolved, so future calls are not assumed entirely net free cash. A “no deviation” monitoring conclusion is not an audit or a usable-cash reconciliation. [E07,E10; S27.]\\n\\nShort borrowings include callable facilities and current maturities. Margin requirements and <=90-day eligible book debts make reported unused sanctions different from available cash. The note-level tax provision₹2.45 crore, approximate statutory liabilities₹0.39 crore, customer advances₹2.33 crore and other accruals do not perfectly match face totals; the model conservatively pays legacy obligations without automatically monetizing tax assets₹2.94 crore. Existing RIICO/vehicle debt is counted once; current gratuity is settled in the bridge and long legacy gratuity₹0.25 crore before terminal equity. External RDPL guarantee₹4.95 crore is stress-tested separately. [E08–E09; S02 pp145–150.]\\n\\n## 10. Valuation & Market Expectations\\n\\n| Metric | Value |\\n|---|---|\\n| Fullpaid staleclose / lasttrade | 226.35 / 219.00 |\\n| Partlypaid staleclose | 77.85 |\\n| Fullpaidclass marketcap₹cr | 128.0915 |\\n| Partlypaidclass marketcap₹cr | 20.5524 |\\n| Observedbothclasses equity₹cr | 148.6439 |\\n| Fullpaidparity syntheticcurrentequity₹cr | 166.7279 |\\n| Futurecalls₹cr | 21.12, not currentcash |\\n| Observedclasses / FY26reportedPAT | 14.67 |\\n| MechanicalotherincomeadjustedP/E | 15.13 |\\n| FullyfundedclaimP/Ediagnostic | 18.54 |\\n| StaleMarch EV/operatingEBITDA | 9.76–10.00 |\\n| CurrentEV / reliable normalizedP/E | NS / NS |\\n\\nBoth classes last appeared on16October. ROCKPP ₹77.85 plus an eventual₹80 call is₹157.85, well below the full-paid₹226.35; timing, rights, liquidity and settlement differences prevent an arbitrage conclusion. Full-paid price multiplied by all8.299m issued shares is a **fully-funded claim equivalent**, not current equity market capitalization. The class-summed quoted equity value is a different observation. Reported FY26 EPS₹16.56 uses a weighted denominator of6,118,288 shares; dividing PAT by all8,299,000 fully-called claims gives only about₹12.21 before any return on future call cash. Neither denominator should be silently substituted into the other valuation. The model for a full-paid shareholder uses all existing claims, a dated hypothetical call receipt and **zero new shares from that call**. Future call cash is not added to current EV. [E01,E10,E15; S44 and market_extract.json.]\\n\\nEV diagnostics pair the observed class values with stale March debt/cash. Removing only other income does not solve unaudited profit or stock valuation; reliable normalized P/E and current EV remain NS. No FY27 quarterly cash flow or overlapping TTM is invented.\\n\\n**Execution audit:** full-paid records exist for53/60 expected sessions and17/20 recent sessions. Missing rows are ND, not known zero-trade days. Observed60-session mean/median traded value is approximately₹15.8564/₹9.7314 lakh; median volume4,750 shares. Full-paid lot250 costs₹56,587.50 at the stale close; the ₹5 lakh reference rounds up to9lots/2,250shares/₹509,287.50. Depth, spreads, delivery turnover, circuit availability and future buyer interest are not verified. [E15,E19; NSE source hashes and rows in market_extract.json.]\\n\\n| Participation | Whole lots/day | Sessions for9lots | Meaning |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| 2.00% | 0 | Not feasible | No whole lot feasible at this conservative observed participation |\\n| 5.00% | 0 | Not feasible | No whole lot feasible at this conservative observed participation |\\n| 10.00% | 1 | 9 | Observedonlylower-bound diagnostic, not execution guarantee |\\n\\n**Cash-linked scenarios, not predictions:** audited March31 stocks are carried through seven dated bridge intervals to October19, then60 monthly intervals. Eight intraperiod collection/payment cycles distinguish supplier funding before collection; four/sixteen-cycle sensitivities test discretization. Every payment can trigger bounded funding before the payment, never after a prior breach. Bank financing is uncommitted, cumulative gross-capped at₹2 crore; rescue equity is capped at₹15 crore gross and authorized share headroom, issued in **whole shares**, at25% discount with3% fee. Equity rescue cannot invent pre-entry shares contrary to the current master. Interest, principal, legacy tax/statutory/accrual payments, already-issued rights, project cash and dilution are cash-linked. Post-breach continuation is explicitly an unfunded counterfactual, never an ordinary equity return.\\n\\nBase assumptions are20% sales growth,11% operating EBITDA,65 DSO/175 inventory days/22 payable days. Bear:5%/7.5%/95/220/15 with markdowns and70% rollover. Bull:30%/13%/50/140/30, with lower markdowns. These are analyst assumptions, not guidance. Future rights call₹21.12 crore is assumed collected7April2027 only in the main conditional path, less₹0.43 crore assumed residual issue fees and₹4.82 crore ring-fenced for the remaining e-waste project; zero/half/late calls are tested. Existing designated₹3.99 crore and newly ring-fenced call cash fund only e-waste capex after the assumed maturity date, reducing the remaining₹8.81 crore project requirement; it never becomes free operating cash. Grocery capex₹0.50 crore is explicit. The UAE loan is eliminated within the group, not charged twice.\\n\\n| Bridge / case / finance | Survives | First failure | Bank₹cr | Equity₹cr | Terminal₹ | 5yIRR |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| earmarked_allocation/bear/no_optional_finance | False | 2026-07-03 operating_cash_cost | 0.00 | 0.00 | NS | NS |\\n| earmarked_allocation/bear/bounded_optional | False | 2026-11-19 debt_principal_assumed | 2.00 | 15.00 | NS | NS |\\n| earmarked_allocation/base/no_optional_finance | False | 2026-07-03 operating_cash_cost | 0.00 | 0.00 | NS | NS |\\n| earmarked_allocation/base/bounded_optional | False | 2030-04-11 operating_cash_cost | 2.00 | 15.00 | NS | NS |\\n| earmarked_allocation/bull/no_optional_finance | False | 2026-07-03 operating_cash_cost | 0.00 | 0.00 | NS | NS |\\n| earmarked_allocation/bull/bounded_optional | False | 2031-10-19 terminal_all_debt_settlement | 2.00 | 15.00 | NS | NS |\\n| cash_unavailable/bear/no_optional_finance | False | 2026-03-31 assumed_opening_usable_cash_from_audited_total | 0.00 | 0.00 | NS | NS |\\n| cash_unavailable/bear/bounded_optional | False | 2026-03-31 assumed_opening_usable_cash_from_audited_total | 0.00 | 0.00 | NS | NS |\\n| cash_unavailable/base/no_optional_finance | False | 2026-03-31 assumed_opening_usable_cash_from_audited_total | 0.00 | 0.00 | NS | NS |\\n| cash_unavailable/base/bounded_optional | False | 2026-03-31 assumed_opening_usable_cash_from_audited_total | 0.00 | 0.00 | NS | NS |\\n| cash_unavailable/bull/no_optional_finance | False | 2026-03-31 assumed_opening_usable_cash_from_audited_total | 0.00 | 0.00 | NS | NS |\\n| cash_unavailable/bull/bounded_optional | False | 2026-03-31 assumed_opening_usable_cash_from_audited_total | 0.00 | 0.00 | NS | NS |\\n| deposit_only_restricted/bear/no_optional_finance | False | 2026-10-22 operating_cash_cost | 0.00 | 0.00 | NS | NS |\\n| deposit_only_restricted/bear/bounded_optional | False | 2026-11-19 debt_principal_assumed | 2.00 | 15.00 | NS | NS |\\n| deposit_only_restricted/base/no_optional_finance | False | 2026-10-30 operating_cash_cost | 0.00 | 0.00 | NS | NS |\\n| deposit_only_restricted/base/bounded_optional | False | 2030-04-11 operating_cash_cost | 2.00 | 15.00 | NS | NS |\\n| deposit_only_restricted/bull/no_optional_finance | False | 2031-04-22 operating_cash_cost | 0.00 | 0.00 | NS | NS |\\n| deposit_only_restricted/bull/bounded_optional | False | 2031-10-19 terminal_all_debt_settlement | 2.00 | 15.00 | NS | NS |\\n\\nFirst failures before entry indicate that **the chosen underidentified bridge cannot fund itself**, not that a listed company actually defaulted. A path without a historical cash breach may still fail terminal debt/gratuity/reserve conditions. Failure retains the entire chronology but suppresses ordinary terminal price and IRR. No probability-weighted recommendation is constructed from unsupported weights.\\n\\nTerminal operating value is continuing FCFF/(discount−growth), not an arbitrary P/E. Next-year maintenance/growth investment, working capital, minimum economic reinvestment and permanent cash growth are funded. All debt and legacy gratuity are paid before equity; a full continuation year tests peak cash drawdown. Only cash above the retained reserve is distributable. Debt is not subtracted twice; restricted cash remaining is not automatically added; no dividend is counted both in cash and return. Entry1% and exit2% transaction/slippage haircuts are analytical assumptions, not executable cost estimates. A path must also collect the full existing rights call before ordinary equal-share terminal valuation is allowed.\\n\\n| Test | Survives | First failure | Equity₹cr | Terminal₹ | IRR |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| margin_15 | True | None | 14.71 | 207.06 | -2.36% |\\n| margin_20 | True | None | 2.45 | 349.45 | 8.42% |\\n| margin_25 | True | None | 1.65 | 490.04 | 16.00% |\\n| growth_10 | True | None | 3.81 | 94.66 | -16.51% |\\n| growth_30 | False | 2028-08-22 operating_cash_cost | 15.00 | NS | NS |\\n| dso_45 | False | 2031-10-19 terminal_all_debt_settlement | 15.00 | NS | NS |\\n| dso_90 | False | 2028-11-19 operating_cash_cost | 15.00 | NS | NS |\\n| inventory_120 | True | None | 11.65 | 148.16 | -8.68% |\\n| inventory_220 | False | 2028-03-22 operating_cash_cost | 15.00 | NS | NS |\\n| bank_cap_zero | False | 2026-07-03 operating_cash_cost | 0.00 | NS | NS |\\n| rollover_80pct | False | 2030-01-26 operating_cash_cost | 15.00 | NS | NS |\\n| rights_zero | False | 2028-04-15 operating_cash_cost | 15.00 | NS | NS |\\n| rights_half | False | 2029-02-19 earmarked_project_capex_from_free_cash | 15.00 | NS | NS |\\n| rights_late | False | 2030-03-22 operating_cash_cost | 15.00 | NS | NS |\\n| call_fee_80lakh | False | 2030-03-26 operating_cash_cost | 15.00 | NS | NS |\\n| call_ringfence_zero | False | 2030-04-11 operating_cash_cost | 15.00 | NS | NS |\\n| trading_costs_5pct | True | None | 2.45 | 349.45 | 6.91% |\\n| early_collections_50pct | False | 2030-03-12 operating_cash_cost | 15.00 | NS | NS |\\n| cycles_4 | False | 2030-03-26 operating_cash_cost | 15.00 | NS | NS |\\n| cycles_16 | False | 2030-04-17 operating_cash_cost | 15.00 | NS | NS |\\n| guarantee_4_95 | False | 2029-09-26 operating_cash_cost | 15.00 | NS | NS |\\n| inventory_markdown_20 | False | 2029-06-03 operating_cash_cost | 15.00 | NS | NS |\\n| related_ar_markdown_50 | False | 2027-03-01 operating_cash_cost | 15.00 | NS | NS |\\n| terminal_k_13 | True | None | 2.45 | 442.04 | 13.64% |\\n| terminal_k_19 | True | None | 2.45 | 291.58 | 4.56% |\\n| terminal_g_zero | True | None | 2.45 | 347.01 | 8.27% |\\n| terminal_roic_10 | True | None | 2.45 | 310.66 | 5.90% |\\n\\n| Variable | Target | Threshold | Status |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| margin | price_only | 15.88% | Bracketed diagnostic threshold, not a forecast |\\n| margin | hurdle12 | 22.20% | Bracketed diagnostic threshold, not a forecast |\\n| margin | hurdle15 | 24.26% | Bracketed diagnostic threshold, not a forecast |\\n| margin | hurdle18 | 26.54% | Bracketed diagnostic threshold, not a forecast |\\n| margin | 5x | 48.79% | Bracketed diagnostic threshold, not a forecast |\\n| margin | 10x | NS | No crossing found within disclosed range; no extrapolated root |\\n| growth | price_only | 10.19% | Bracketed diagnostic threshold, not a forecast |\\n| growth | hurdle12 | NS | No crossing found within disclosed range; no extrapolated root |\\n| growth | hurdle15 | NS | No crossing found within disclosed range; no extrapolated root |\\n| growth | hurdle18 | NS | No crossing found within disclosed range; no extrapolated root |\\n| growth | 5x | NS | No crossing found within disclosed range; no extrapolated root |\\n| growth | 10x | NS | No crossing found within disclosed range; no extrapolated root |\\n\\nReverse grids test all disclosed points before bracketing crossings; failed or below-target trials are retained. Margin roots hold base working-capital assumptions; growth roots use a separately labelled cash-disciplined16% margin/50DSO/140inventory-day/30payable-day branch. This is not a hidden optimistic base case. A root may be a funding-feasibility boundary rather than a smooth valuation equality. “No crossing” means no solution in the displayed domain, not proof that a solution cannot exist elsewhere. Required high EBITDA margins must be compared with FY26 gross margin of about23.81% and operating EBITDA of about13.59%, not with the anomalous Dubai PAT margin.\\n\\n| Conditional case | 12%hurdle | 15%hurdle | 18%hurdle |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| margin_15 | 114.00 | 99.89 | 87.82 |\\n| margin_20 | 192.40 | 168.58 | 148.21 |\\n| margin_25 | 269.80 | 236.40 | 207.84 |\\n\\nHurdle-entry prices are conditional on the displayed economic and financing policy, not buy levels. They keep the rescue issue-price policy anchored to the observed full-paid price; reducing an investor's purchase price does not magically improve the company's financing terms. All cash, financing, sensitivity and reverse outputs come from the same canonical model. Retention: **189 requests, 189 unique complete paths, 48 failed unique paths**.\\n\\n## 11. Bear Case & Permanent-Loss Path\\n\\nThe strongest bear case is a feedback loop: stock ages or needs discounting, related/old debtors do not pay, eligible collateral falls, banks reduce drawing power, and operating cash becomes insufficient before sales can be collected. Calling rights or raising discounted equity takes time and may not be fully subscribed. External guarantee invocation adds another cash claim. If refinancing only delays an inventory realization loss, book equity does not protect per-share value. A stale SME quotation can prevent a timely exit even before a formal insolvency event. [E06–E10,E19–E20.]\\n\\nIllustratively, a20% haircut on₹50.03 crore inventory is about₹10 crore; a50% loss on the₹9.103 crore RDPL debtor is about₹4.55 crore, before the₹4.95 crore guarantee, taxes and liquidation costs. These are distinct stress channels, not a blanket claim that all exposures fail simultaneously or that guarantees are already debt. No unsupported asset-recovery price floor is inferred from the₹71.31 crore reported equity. The model additionally tests broad receivable haircuts, which are intentionally harsher than a debtor-specific loss. Bear paths without financing do not acquire a finite ordinary return merely because a terminal multiple can be calculated.\\n\\nThesis-break variable: **cash collection and saleable inventory realization relative to cash claims**, rather than reported revenue or EBITDA alone. An independent audited component and a clean cash/guarantee roll-forward can improve the assessment; a further acceleration in aged debtors, stock and funding reliance would worsen it.\\n\\n## 12. 5× / 10× Feasibility\\n\\n**Classification: Speculative / unsupported.**\\n\\nFivefold and tenfold five-year outcomes require approximately37.97% and58.49% annualized wealth growth, respectively, before recognizing the assumed trading-cost drag. The reverse tests explicitly target those net wealth multiples and retain failed roots. A higher headline revenue CAGR alone does not achieve them: working capital, tax, project investment, dilution and terminal reinvestment consume the incremental economics.\\n\\nAt a 20% volume/value growth assumption, pushing EBITDA to margins well above current gross-margin economics is not a credible base route to such outcomes. Growth-root tests deliberately improve cash discipline but still pay claims and fund continuation. A “no crossing within range” result is more useful than manufacturing a10× target with an unearned exit multiple. A ten-year 5×/10× return illustration would require further evidence on durable economics; no ten-year operating forecast is invented. The group is small enough to grow substantially, but size is optionality, not proof of high-quality compounding.\\n\\n## 13. Monitoring Plan\\n\\n| Trigger / evidence needed | What would change the assessment | Review timing |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Dubai independent audit and revenue/profit/tax/receivable reconciliation | Verify the material component, its unusual margin and cash remittance ability | Before G2 can clear; at next annual/interim filing |\\n| Bank/FD roll-forward reconciling₹5.059 cash,₹1.58 deposits,₹4.30 FD and₹3.99 designated balance | Establish unrestricted cash, funding location and project money without double counting | Before underwriting current liquidity |\\n| RDPL debtor ageing, settlement receipts and guarantee confirmations | Demonstrate cash recovery; reconcile external₹4.95 and subsidiary₹8 guarantees versus note total | Next results and material RPT updates |\\n| SKU ageing, NRV/write-off cohorts and stock-to-PPE documentation | Explain₹8.58 capitalization, depreciation, actual cashcapex and saleability | Before CAP/EARN scoreability improves |\\n| Call notice, subscription/cash receipt and security conversion | Replace hypothetical call timing/collection with verified events | At each rights announcement |\\n| Eligible borrowing base, facility renewal, covenants and legacy-dues settlement | Distinguish nominal sanctions from drawable cash | At each funding result/renewal |\\n| E-waste commissioning, permits, throughput and project returns | Evidence for a new revenue/capital cohort rather than a narrative | At commissioning and first two operating periods |\\n| Store openings, closures, same-store sales and cash contribution | Test November2026 store ambition and new grocery economics | At original deadline, not prematurely |\\n| Full primary tape, circuits, live depth and actual lot-capacity | Reassess G4 at the same₹5lakh reference and2/5/10% participation | Before any transaction decision |\\n\\nA price decline alone changes prospective IAS inputs, not BQS/GQS. If data resolve, recalculate the original canonical factors, document each change and preserve the prior vintage. No silent score upgrade or ranking insertion is permitted.\\n\\n## 14. Final Classification & Conditional Action\\n\\n**Evidence Hold; research incomplete on material reliability, current funding and tradability.** BQS/GQS/IAS remain NS with transparent bounds. The v3 classification matrix cannot create a favorable category from an incomplete composite, and compounder criteria are not met. This is not an “Avoid on proven fraud” judgment and not a solvency declaration. It is a refusal to underwrite what is not yet supported.\\n\\nConditional action is to resolve the evidence in Section13 and rerun the same model, not trade on the cheapest apparent P/E. Even if reported growth persists, funded cash conversion and intrinsic value **per fully diluted, fully-called share** must improve. No position size or portfolio role is recommended. In a multi-company SME page, retain the identical framework structure, show the Hold conspicuously, and exclude this incomplete issuer from numeric investability ranks rather than assigning an artificial neutral score.\\n\\n## 15. Sources, Assumptions & Open Questions\\n\\nEvidence IDs E01–E20 are in evidence_ledger.json with type V verified disclosure, C calculation, M management statement or A analyst assumption, source locator, confidence and limitation. Primary source byte hashes identify the inspected documents; they do not certify the truth of disclosures. Public source links follow. Original PDFs, OCR, screenshots, service helpers and private upload identifiers are intentionally excluded from the reconstructable research bundles. Concise authored facts and executable calculations remain.\\n\\n| ID | Primary document |\\n|---|---|\\n| S01 | [Capacity addition 16 Oct - Rockingdeals will launch its first Retail Grocery Mart at Sarai on 20 October 2026, with ₹50 lakh investment.](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/rockingdeals_16102026134731_Retail_Expansion_RDCL.pdf) |\\n| S02 | [Annual Report 2026](https://archives.nseindia.com/annual_reports/SME_AR_31795_ROCKINGDCE_2025_2026_A_9082837_07092026205811.pdf) |\\n| S03 | [Annual Report 2025](https://archives.nseindia.com/annual_reports/SME_AR_29118_ROCKINGDCE_2024_2025_A_12716460_17092025171647.pdf) |\\n| S04 | [Annual Report 2024](https://archives.nseindia.com/annual_reports/SME_AR_26314_ROCKINGDCE_2023_2024_A_05102024070034.pdf) |\\n| S05 | [Un-audited-Financial-Results-for-Q1-ending-30.06.2025](https://rdcel.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/Un-audited-Financial-Results-for-Q1-ending-30.06.2025.pdf) |\\n| S06 | [Annual Report FY 2019-2020](https://rdcel.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/Annual-Report-2019-20.pdf) |\\n| S07 | [Annual Report FY 2020-2021](https://rdcel.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/Annual-Report-20-21.pdf) |\\n| S08 | [Annual Report FY 2021-2022](https://rdcel.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/Annual-Report-21-22.pdf) |\\n| S09 | [Annual Report FY 2022-2023](https://rdcel.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/Annual-Report-22-23-RDCEL.pdf) |\\n| S10 | [Annual Report FY 2024-25](https://rdcel.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/Annual-Report-FY-2024-25.pdf) |\\n| S11 | [Annual report 2025-26](https://rdcel.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Annual-report-Rockingdeals-Final-V4.pdf) |\\n| S12 | [Intimation to Stock Exchange regarding MOU signed between Etlus Mode Private Limited and Company](https://rdcel.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/Stock-Exchange-Intimation-regarding-MOU-between-Eltus-and-RDCEL-1.pdf) |\\n| S13 | [Trade Agreement with PUMA](https://rdcel.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/Trade-AGreement-with-PUMA.pdf) |\\n| S14 | [MOU signed between Oriana and The Company](https://rdcel.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/IntimationtostockexchangeregardingMOUsignedbetweenOrianaandRDCEL.pdf) |\\n| S15 | [Intimation regarding joint venture agreement signed with Digi2L](https://rdcel.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/SE-Intimation-joint-venture-agreement-signed-Digi2L-and-RDCEL.pdf) |\\n| S16 | [Press Release-Incorporation of a Subsidiary Company](https://rdcel.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/Pressreleasesubsidiary.pdf) |\\n| S17 | [Intimation regarding resignation of Tarun goel](https://rdcel.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/Intimation-regarding-resignation-of-Tarun-goel.pdf) |\\n| S18 | [Intimation regarding resignation of CFO-Mr. Jitender Verma](https://rdcel.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/Intimation-regarding-resignation-of-CFO-Mr.-Jitender-Verma.pdf) |\\n| S19 | [STatement of Deviation for the Half Year ended on Sep 2024](https://rdcel.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/STatement-of-Deviation-for-the-Half-Year-ended-on-Sep-2024.pdf) |\\n| S20 | [STatement of Deviation for the Half Year ended on March 2025](https://rdcel.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/STatement-of-Deviation-for-the-Half-Year-ended-on-March-2025.pdf) |\\n| S21 | [H2 FY24 Earning Call Transcript](https://rdcel.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/Rockingdeals-Circular-Economy-Limited-Conference-call-Transcript.pdf) |\\n| S22 | [H1 FY25 Earning Call Transcript](https://rdcel.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/Transcript-.pdf) |\\n| S23 | [Investor presentation for H2 and FY 23-24](https://rdcel.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/IntimationtostockechangeregardingInvestorpresentationforH2FY.pdf) |\\n| S24 | [Investor Presentation for H1 FY 24-25](https://rdcel.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/InvestorPresentationforH1FY24-25.pdf) |\\n| S25 | [Letter of Offer Dated: October 13, 2025](https://rdcel.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/Letter-of-Offer.pdf) |\\n| S26 | [RHP](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/emerge/corporates/content/RockingDeals_RHP.pdf) |\\n| S27 | [monitor_Mar26](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/rockingdeals_15052026202555_intimation_of_Submission_of_monitoring_agency__1_.pdf) |\\n| S28 | [expansion_Dec24](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/rockingdeals_30122024105338_businessupdaterdcel.pdf) |\\n| S29 | [withdraw_Mar25](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/rockingdeals_21032025110320_intimationrdcel.pdf) |\\n| S30 | [peer_newjaisa_FY26](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/NEWJAISA2020_27052026195417_OutcomeBM_Financialsresults.pdf) |\\n| S31 | [peer_liquidity_FY25](https://investors.liquidityservices.com/static-files/5bce319c-3b64-46e4-a77e-5560a376ab64) |\\n| S32 | [rockingdeals_10112025165856_outcomeofbmeeting](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/rockingdeals_10112025165856_outcomeofbmeeting.pdf) |\\n| S33 | [rockingdeals_11102025154341_OutcomeofBMdated11102025](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/rockingdeals_11102025154341_OutcomeofBMdated11102025.pdf) |\\n| S34 | [rockingdeals_13012025143507_businessupdate](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/rockingdeals_13012025143507_businessupdate.pdf) |\\n| S35 | [rockingdeals_13032025113858_intimationmou](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/rockingdeals_13032025113858_intimationmou.pdf) |\\n| S36 | [rockingdeals_13072026190944_Intimation_under_regulation_30](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/rockingdeals_13072026190944_Intimation_under_regulation_30.pdf) |\\n| S37 | [rockingdeals_15092026154531_Intimation_to_Stock_Exchange-final](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/rockingdeals_15092026154531_Intimation_to_Stock_Exchange-final.pdf) |\\n| S38 | [rockingdeals_16022026160123_Intimation_for_Strategic_collab__1_](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/rockingdeals_16022026160123_Intimation_for_Strategic_collab__1_.pdf) |\\n| S39 | [rockingdeals_22082025164108_Intimationofloan](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/rockingdeals_22082025164108_Intimationofloan.pdf) |\\n| S40 | [rockingdeals_22082025164426_conversionofunsecuredloan](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/rockingdeals_22082025164426_conversionofunsecuredloan.pdf) |\\n| S41 | [rockingdeals_24062026155237_Update_RDCEL_FS](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/rockingdeals_24062026155237_Update_RDCEL_FS.pdf) |\\n| S42 | [rockingdeals_25112025204640_Outcome_BM_Allotment](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/rockingdeals_25112025204640_Outcome_BM_Allotment.pdf) |\\n| S43 | [rockingdeals_27052026175459_Outcome_of_the_BM](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/rockingdeals_27052026175459_Outcome_of_the_BM.pdf) |\\n| S44 | [rockingdeals_28112025135734_Intimation_Rockingdeals_Revised](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/rockingdeals_28112025135734_Intimation_Rockingdeals_Revised.pdf) |\\n| S45 | [rockingdeals_29032025160327_intimationofwos](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/rockingdeals_29032025160327_intimationofwos.pdf) |\\n| S46 | [rockingdeals_30052025163719_Outcomeofbmrdcel](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/rockingdeals_30052025163719_Outcomeofbmrdcel.pdf) |\\n| S47 | [rockingdeals_31072026115219_Reply_to_NSE__1_](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/rockingdeals_31072026115219_Reply_to_NSE__1_.pdf) |\\n| S48 | [team_sandeshc_17062026125140_21](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/team_sandeshc_17062026125140_21.pdf) |\\n| S49 | [GNG Electronics FY26 primary earnings release, 5 May 2026](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/GNGNSE_05052026194354_Press_Release_FY26_Financial_Results.pdf) |\\n\\nMaterial unresolved questions are the Dubai audit/segment-profit bridge, original/restated history, stock capitalization and cashcapex, available cash/FD reconciliation, guarantee roll-forward, old debtor collection, tax/other-current-liability note mismatches, current borrowing eligibility and complete market executability. A GNG annual-report BSE request returned403; its official earnings release supplies the limited peer comparison. The NSE announcements API returned an empty result despite known filings; it was not treated as evidence of no disclosures. Issuer and exchange-linked filings supplied the audit history and later updates.\\n\\n**Reproducibility:** report, score audit, market audit, financial metrics, all scenario/reverse/sensitivity results and full path archive regenerate offline from the authored inputs and code in either compact or full package. Reconstruction deletes every generated output and attestation first, checks authored hashes against a separately retained baseline, rebuilds with standard-library Python, independently verifies invariants and compares all regenerated hashes. Neither bundle needs the other, raw PDFs or service access. Current owned Library download/readback proofs are held separately, not self-certified inside these files.\\n\\n**All16 final checks, with unresolved evidence visible:**\\n\\n| # | Check | Status | Disposition |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| 1 | Identity / security / dates | PASS | Exactissuer, twoISINclasses, fullpaidquote16Oct vs cutoff19Oct, groupandhistoricalscope explicit. |\\n| 2 | Primary records and attribution | PASS | Originalaudits,annexures,notes,rights,monitoring,executionandpeersread; M/A distinctfromV. |\\n| 3 | Audit / RPT / financing / dilution | PASS with G2/G3 HOLD | Noadverseopinioninvented; materialreliabilityandfundinggapsretained; rightsalreadyissued. |\\n| 4 | Periods and units | PASS | RHP sourcehundreds convertedtocrore; nooverlappingTTM; noFY27quarterlyCFinvented. |\\n| 5 | Revenue decomposition | UNRESOLVED / NS | Nominalgrowthandgroup/standalonebridgecomputed; noverifiedprice-volume-acquisitionorganicseries. |\\n| 6 | Cash conversion | PASS with caveat | CFO/PATratioofsums; cashcapexnoncashreclassificationdoesnotexcuseoperatingcashdeficit. |\\n| 7 | Incremental return | UNRESOLVED / NS | 3y/5ysameandlaggedproxiesexplicit; cohortandorganiccapitalunavailable. |\\n| 8 | Normalization / taxes / claims | PASS with G2 HOLD | Otherincomeremovedmechanically, notfraudadjusted; currentclaimsandfuturecallsseparate; allgroupdebt/taxcounted. |\\n| 9 | Scenarios reconcile | PASS | Datedpreentry/monthly/intraperiodfunding, boundedwhole-sharerescue, rights andrestrictedcash countedonce; terminalcapital/reserveearned. |\\n| 10 | Canonical weights | PASS | Exactly13factors; weights100%; QusesfinalunroundedBQS, whichisNS. |\\n| 11 | Missing evidence | PASS | NSnull, coverageandbounds; nozeroorneutralimputation. |\\n| 12 | Overrides | PASS | PersistentCASHcap5onlyonGQS; nofabricated3cap; NSnotconvertedtonumber. |\\n| 13 | Classification | PASS | EvidenceHoldprecedesmatrix; nocompounderclaim. |\\n| 14 | Liquidity | UNRESOLVED / NS | ₹5lakhreference; integral250sharelots; observed53/60notzero-filled; noexecutabilityclaim. |\\n| 15 | Bear / falsification | PASS | Ageddebtor/stock/guarantee/renewalchainanddatedconditions explicit. |\\n| 16 | Ranking | PASS | Commonv3; incompleteissuerexcludedfromnumericranking; noimputedscoretie. |\\n\\nUpdate log: initial v3 deep-research record at20October2026 cutoff; source retrieval and mechanical verification performed afterward without using post-cutoff operating developments. Gates, factors, cash constraints, current-class valuation, named primary peers and execution audit were completed before artifact freeze. A successful reconstruction proves reproducibility, not investability or the truth of unaudited accounts.\\n\"","\"number\"","\"content_markdown\"","\"Executive Verdict\"","\"\\n**Research incomplete / evidence Hold. BQS NS · GQS NS · IAS NS. Not a compounder designation or an investable ranking.** The reported growth is real as a disclosure, but its organic economics, cash realization and consolidated profit reliability are not established sufficiently to underwrite a five-year return. The observed full-paid share close is ₹226.35 on **16 October 2026**, not a verified executable 19 October price.\\n\\nThree findings dominate. First, the FY26 consolidated auditor relied on management-supplied, unaudited Dubai accounts contributing ₹4.0059 crore, about 40% of reported group PAT. The opinion remains unmodified; this is **not** an adverse opinion or a fraud finding. Second, FY25 stock worth ₹8.58 crore was capitalized into fixed assets, while reported cash-flow capex presents a much larger gross outflow than the economic cash movement can safely establish. Third, group FY26 CFO was approximately **negative ₹21.43 crore**, with ₹50.03 crore inventory and ₹19.71 crore receivables. These matters outweigh a superficially modest reported P/E. [E02–E07; FY26 annual report pp127–128,139,151–153; FY25 pp73–74.](https://archives.nseindia.com/annual_reports/SME_AR_31795_ROCKINGDCE_2025_2026_A_9082837_07092026205811.pdf)\\n\\nA future ₹21.12 crore rights call is neither current cash nor a new-share allotment. Existing partly-paid shares already bring total issued claims to 8,299,000, against 5,659,000 currently fully-paid shares. Funding, minority treatment and valuation must preserve both classes. No purchase, sale or allocation is proposed. The useful next step is reconciliation, not a price target.\\n\\n\"","\"Research Header, Gates and Data Confidence\"","\"\\n- Exact issuer: Rockingdeals Circular Economy Limited, formerly Technix Electronics Limited/Private Limited. NSE Emerge **ROCKINGDCE**, ISIN **INE0PTR01012**, series SM; separate partly-paid **ROCKPP**, ISIN **IN90PTR01010**, series E1.\\n- Information cutoff: **20 October 2026, Asia/Kolkata**. Market window ends **19 October 2026**. Later information is not used to judge prior promises.\\n- Framework: supplied **v3.0, updated 3 October 2026**; exact source hash in framework_provenance.json. Deep research with unresolved evidence, not a completed favorable recommendation.\\n- Scope: consolidated latest accounts; original standalone and RHP-restated history kept separate. Indian Accounting Standards under the Companies Act, **not assumed Ind AS**. Currency ₹ crore except explicit per-share/lot amounts; RHP historical source units are hundreds of rupees.\\n- Horizon: five years from 19 October 2026. Nominal equity hurdle **15%**, sensitivities 12% and 18%, before investor taxes. Terminal operating discount rate 16%, independent of the equity hurdle; 13% and 19% sensitivities. Reference liquidity position ₹5 lakh is an illustration, not a suggested investment.\\n\\n| Gate | Status | Reason |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| G1 | Conditional pass / monitor | Unmodified opinions and no substantiated integrity failure; material transparency/related-entity concerns remain. |\\n| G2 | HOLD | Material unauditedDubaiPAT; original/restatedvintages; restrictedcash/guarantee/segment/cashcapex conflicts. No favorableinvestmentconclusion. |\\n| G3 | HOLD | Currentfunding and usablecash cannot be verified; model failures are conditional, not evidence of actual default. |\\n| G4 | HOLD | Observed5%participation cannot fit1lot; incomplete60sessionrowcoverage and unverifieddepth/restrictions. |\\n\\nConfidence is **Low overall**, set by the weakest material area: usable cash, unaudited component earnings and comparable organic economics. Identity and retrieved price/lot facts have High retrieval confidence; ordinary historical headline values and governance assessment Medium; growth attribution, capital cohorts, current funding and valuation Low. A known arithmetic identity is not evidence that the underlying economic claim is reliable.\\n\\n\"","\"Scorecard and Calculation Audit\"","\"\\nScores use the exact v3 factors. BQS weights: GOV20%, MOAT20%, REV12%, DUR10%, CAP12%, CASH10%, BAL8%, EXEC8%. GQS: REV40%, DUR20%, EARN15%, CAP15%, CASH10%. IAS: final unrounded BQS25%, R35%, D20%, **P price-implied expectations10%**, L10%. No factor is replaced by a probability estimate. Canonical factors are reused, never rescored for a different composite.\\n\\n| Composite | Final | Coverage | Known subtotal | Raw range | Cap | Effective range |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| BQS | NS | 76.00% | 2.4800 | 2.7200–4.8800 | None | 2.7200–4.8800 |\\n| GQS | NS | 30.00% | 1.0000 | 1.7000–8.0000 | 5 | 1.7000–5.0000 |\\n| IAS | NS | 20.00% | 0.6000 | 1.4000–8.6000 | None | 1.4000–8.6000 |\\n\\n| Factor | Score | Confidence | Evidence | Basis | Contrary / limitation |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| GOV | 4 | Medium | E02,E03,E08,E14 | Unmodified opinions and disclosure of unusual inventory capitalization are positives, but repeated presentation/cash/guarantee inconsistencies and material unaudited component limit minority-owner confidence. | No established diversion, fraud or adverse opinion; do not substitute concerns for a substantiated G1 failure. |\\n| MOAT | 3 | Medium | E13,E20,E16 | Procurement/disposal relationships and secondary sales network create practical access; no demonstrated exclusivity, switching costs or durable abnormal cash returns. | Brand contracts exist; sourcing knowledge can have local value despite absent quantified retention. |\\n| REV | NS | Low | E04,E17 | Nominal growth is strong but related-party, restatement, geography and new-perimeter shifts prevent verified5yorganic/3yfallback measure. | Standalone FY24–26 still grows sharply; uncertainty is attribution, not denial of reported sales. |\\n| DUR | 4 | Medium | E12,E13,E20 | Excess-stock/reuse demand is persistent, but profitable scale requires inventory funding and new geography/project execution. | Large addressable reverse-commerce flows and successful peers show possible runway. |\\n| CAP | NS | Low | E03,E11,E17 | No clean comparable organic incremental invested-capital/commissioning cohorts; inventory-to-PPE and inherited land debt break naive ROIC comparisons. | Closing ratios and operatingICproxies are disclosed as diagnostics rather than hidden. |\\n| CASH | 2 | Medium | E05,E06,E07 | Two large cashabsorbing years acrossFY24–26, receivables/inventory growth and weak funding-quality evidence persist despiteFY25positiveCFO. | FY25CFOpositive; some cash absorption can fund profitable future growth, but realization unverified. |\\n| BAL | 3 | Medium | E07,E08,E09 | Large working-capital exposure, uncertain availablecash, callableborrowings and externalguarantee weaken resilience. | Reported equity exceeds debt and auditors report no default; does not prove neartermcashavailability. |\\n| EXEC | 3 | Medium | E12,E14,E04 | Promised expansion/JV subsequently withdrawn and e-waste use remains small; disclosure corrections and CFOturnover add execution risk. | Revenue scale increased and Dubaisubsidiary became active; attribution and cashverification remain weak. |\\n| EARN | NS | Low | E02,E03,E17 | Dubai72.85%reportedPATmargin and40%groupPAT unaudited; historicalreclassification undermines clean earningsquality persistence. | Group opinion unmodified and standalonePATpositive; no unsupportedwriteoff of all earnings. |\\n| R | NS | Low | E07,E18 | No defensiblecurrentbaseIRR with unknownbridgefunding/reliability; failedpathsnotconvertedtoordinaryreturns. | Conditional improvedeconomicpaths can earnterminalvalue, without makingthem probable. |\\n| D | 3 | Medium | E06,E08,E19 | Agedrelateddebtor/inventorymarkdown, externalguarantee and impairedliquidity create permanentloss channels. | Assetbacking and uncalledrights provide potentialbuffers if collected/realized. |\\n| P | NS | Low | E02,E07,E18 | Price-implied expectations cannot be scored confidently: reverse operating-margin, sales and funding requirements depend on unresolved cash and group earnings, even though conditional thresholds are calculated. | Cash-linked reverse grids and terminal sensitivities show what the stale full-paid quote would require; unverified assumptions are not automatically cheapness. |\\n| L | NS | Low | E15,E19 | Observedconservative5%volume admitszero250sharelots; missing7sessions and no depth/circuits preventcompleteexecutionrating. | 53 primarysessionsandcurrentmasterallowboundedobservedliquiditydiagnostics; notpurelysecondaryND. |\\n\\nFor known weighted subtotal K and missing weight m, bounds are K+m to K+10m. Missing values remain NS, not zero, 5 or a reweighted average. Persistent CASH=2 caps GQS at 5; it does not make an incomplete GQS numerically scoreable. No substantiated G1 or G3 failure has been established, so no unsupported governance/solvency cap of 3 is applied. G2 Hold blocks a live IAS. Supplementary nested IAS bounds are calculated from BQS bounds in score_audit.json, not presented as a score. R would use supported base-case IRR less the15% equity hurdle and the exact v3 interval schedule; without a defensible supported base return it staysNS. Boundary tests are retained in score_audit.json. All computations use Decimal and presentation-only half-up rounding. No historical factor exceeds the Medium-confidence ceiling of 8.\\n\\n\"","\"Business Overview\"","\"\\nRockingdeals buys and redistributes excess, open-box, refurbished, slow-moving or end-of-life consumer inventory. Its economic opportunity is acquiring stock below realizable selling value and finding secondary demand. The risk is **ownership of uncertain inventory**, not merely operating an asset-light marketplace. A logo or sourcing agreement does not establish exclusivity, recurring volumes, take-or-pay terms, manufacturer buyback, retail warranty coverage or a protected margin. [E13,E20; RHP business description and FY26 inventory/accounting policies.](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/emerge/corporates/content/RockingDeals_RHP.pdf)\\n\\nThe listed issuer must not be confused with similarly named **Rockingdeals Private Limited (RDPL)**, a related external entity and debtor/guarantee beneficiary. Harkishanji Products and AAA Services are also related counterparties, not automatically eliminated group subsidiaries. Current consolidated subsidiaries are Sustainquest Private Limited and Dubai Rocking Deals General Trading LLC, both wholly owned by the latest report. The remaining 42% of Sustainquest was acquired in March 2025; its disclosure says it had no business before acquisition. Earlier Digi2L joint-venture announcements do not establish an ongoing 58% economic stake after the March 2025 termination. No minority interest is assumed in the latest model. [E06,E08,E12; acquisition disclosure S45 and AR26 subsidiary/RPT notes.](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/rockingdeals_29032025160327_intimationofwos.pdf)\\n\\nThe business is expanding beyond its earlier liquidation trade into pre-owned products, Dubai distribution, e-waste and grocery retail. Each adds distinct procurement, collection, compliance or execution requirements. They are not interchangeable “circular economy” revenue streams and no uncommissioned e-waste capacity is granted revenue automatically.\\n\\n\"","\"Moat / Industry Position\"","\"\\nSourcing relationships, knowledge of realizable prices and an outlet/distributor network may produce local access advantages. Demonstrated durable barriers are weaker: no independently verified exclusive supply, customer retention cohorts, stable category-level returns or cash-backed pricing premium. Stocks can become obsolete while accounting value remains unchanged; larger platforms and specialist refurbishers compete for both supply and demand. The lower-of-cost-and-NRV policy is a required valuation convention, not independent evidence of realizable stock value. [E13,E20; S13,S22,S26.]\\n\\n**Named primary economic peers, not an indiscriminate valuation basket:**\\n\\n| Peer | Primary observation | Economic lesson / comparability limit |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| GNG Electronics Limited | FY26 revenue ₹1,891.1 crore, EBITDA ₹200.5 crore, PAT ₹132 crore; 727,000 devices in its release | A much larger ICT refurbisher demonstrates scale and reported ~10.6% EBITDA/~7.0% PAT margins. Product, geography and processing mix differ; annual cash-flow retrieval was incomplete, so no CFO or current peer multiple is invented. [S49](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/GNGNSE_05052026194354_Press_Release_FY26_Financial_Results.pdf) |\\n| Newjaisa Technologies Limited | FY26 revenue ₹40.4882 crore versus ₹65.6551 crore; PAT −₹17.4189 crore versus −₹1.1318 crore | Weaker refurbishment comparator illustrates stock and demand risk. Auditor KAM inventory write-off ₹12.3810 crore differs from broader cash-flow balance-written-off ₹12.4026 crore. It is not an identical mixed-liquidation business. [S30](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/NEWJAISA2020_27052026195417_OutcomeBM_Financialsresults.pdf) |\\n| Liquidity Services, Inc. | FY25 to September: revenue US$476.669m, EBIT US$35.066m, PAT US$28.093m, CFO US$66.758m | Marketplace/consignment economics differ from owned inventory: revenue includes US$310.538m purchase sales and US$166.131m consignment fees. CFO includes US$14.490m stock compensation and US$5.241m inventory release; capex/software US$7.800m and acquisitions US$6.496m. GMV is not revenue, and cash conversion is not all owner cash. [S31](https://investors.liquidityservices.com/static-files/5bce319c-3b64-46e4-a77e-5560a376ab64) |\\n\\nThese disclose a stronger scale comparator, a weaker stock-risk comparator and a differentiated liquidation marketplace. Different currencies, fiscal calendars, acquisitions and gross/net accounting preclude unadjusted multiple transplantation. Their current market EV/P/E values are ND here; this is an economic comparison, not a fabricated peer valuation average.\\n\\n\"","\"Revenue Growth & Scale\"","\"\\n**Originally issued pre-listing history, kept separate from restatements:**\\n\\n| FY | Original revenue₹cr | Original PAT₹cr | Original CFO₹cr | Cash₹cr | Source/scope |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| 2020 | 17.5819906 | 0.1214223 | 2.5847970 | 1.4719608 | Original standalone; FY20 report financial statements |\\n| 2021 | 10.5563213 | 0.0968567 | -1.7833665 | 0.1319961 | Original standalone; FY21 pp20–22 |\\n| 2022 | 14.8281641 | 0.1250846 | NS | 0.3011493 | Original standalone; FY22 pp23–24; no original CF statement |\\n| 2023 | 15.0100814 | 1.4503014 | 0.4188731 | 0.0645216 | Original standalone; FY23 pp8–10 |\\n\\n**Restated/comparative series used for labelled diagnostics:**\\n\\n| FY | Revenue ₹cr | PAT ₹cr | CFO ₹cr | Reported cashcapex ₹cr | Scope |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| 2021 | 10.5563 | -0.0148 | -1.4971 | NS | RHP restated standalone, hundred-rupee source normalized to crore |\\n| 2022 | 14.8282 | 0.1437 | 0.5621 | NS | RHP restated standalone |\\n| 2023 | 15.0101 | 1.5434 | 0.4189 | 0.0946 | RHP restated standalone, differs from later comparative |\\n| 2024 | 49.5612 | 5.2077 | -15.3381 | 1.7867 | Original standalone |\\n| 2025 | 55.4351 | 5.3377 | 2.9957 | 9.9975 | Consolidated comparative in FY26, group newly formed |\\n| 2026 | 119.8364 | 10.1309 | -21.4300 | 3.1200 | Consolidated FY26 annual report, cashflow rounded to lakh |\\n\\nThe table deliberately labels vintages. FY23 RHP PAT ₹1.5433914 crore differs from the later FY24 comparative ₹1.4503 crore; related staff expense differs by approximately ₹0.0931 crore. FY21 originally audited PAT and later comparisons also differ. FY22 originally issued accounts did not contain a cash-flow statement; the CFO shown is a later comparative/restatement. These are not one untouched six-year series. [E04,E17; original reports S06–S09, RHP S26, AR24 S04.]\\n\\n| FY | Revenue addition ₹cr | Reported growth | Organic attribution |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| 2022 | 4.2718 | 40.47% | NS |\\n| 2023 | 0.1819 | 1.23% | NS |\\n| 2024 | 34.5511 | 230.19% | NS |\\n| 2025 | 5.8739 | 11.85% | NS |\\n| 2026 | 64.4013 | 116.17% | NS |\\n\\n| Window | Nominal reported CAGR | Organic CAGR |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| 3 years | 99.86% | NS |\\n| 5 years | 62.56% | NS |\\n| 10 years | NS | NS |\\n\\nFY26 consolidated revenue addition was ₹64.4013 crore. Standalone addition was ₹45.3076 crore; the group-minus-parent revenue difference was ₹19.0937 crore, about 29.65% of group addition. That is a perimeter bridge, **not a claim that the balance was verified organic volume growth**. Group subsidiaries were newly formed/expanded, related-party channels changed, and comparable units, selling prices, category mix, return rates and gross-versus-net policies by stream are unavailable. Standalone FY26 revenue of ₹100.7427 crore is useful corroboration of scale, but not a complete organic decomposition. [E04; S02 p39.]\\n\\nPre-IPO related-party sales were a large share of revenue: approximately 50.19%, 72.90% and 52.99% in FY21–23. The FY24 RPT/customer channel is similarly significant. By FY26 disclosed related sales were much smaller, yet the RDPL receivable increased. Reducing a reported RPT sales percentage does not prove independent sell-through or collection of the legacy debtor. Customer concentration and geographical/category detail are not assumed away. Organic REV remains NS; neither a high nominal CAGR nor a pandemic base solves that attribution problem. [E06,E17; RHP RPT/customer concentration and AR26 RPT notes.]\\n\\n**Latest comparable half-year disclosure and reconciliation:**\\n\\n| Period | Revenue₹cr | PAT₹cr | Vintage/limitation |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| FY25H1 | 21.3635 | 3.1957 | DerivedFY25annual55.4351/5.3377 lessreportedH2revenue34.0716/PAT2.1420; notquarterlyCF |\\n| FY25H2 | 34.0716 | 2.1420 | ComparativeinJune24FY26results |\\n| FY26H1 | 55.0456 | 5.0391 | June24FY26results comparative |\\n| FY26H2 | 64.7909 | 4.8707 | ReportedH2June24FY26results; doesnot sumtoannualPAT owingtoDTA/vintagepresentation |\\n| FY26H2_annual_minus_H1 | 64.7908 | 5.0918 | LatestannualminusH1; derived, notanindependenthalfyearfiling |\\n\\nFY26 H2 reported PAT plus H1 does not equal the full-year figure: the June results leave₹0.2236 crore deferred-tax credit outside the half-year sum, while the later annual report changes full-year PAT by₹0.0025 crore. Latest annual minus H1 is explicitly a derived alternative, not substituted for the filed H2 figure. FY26 H2 reported PAT was below H1 despite higher sales. No H1FY27 results were available within the research cutoff, and annual cash flow is not allocated into invented quarters. [S32,S41,S02.]\\n\\n\"","\"Growth Quality & Reinvestment Runway\"","\"\\nThere is an enduring economic need to clear surplus consumer stock and extend product life. Monetizing that demand profitably requires procurement discipline, saleable quality, warranties, efficient inventory turns, collection and funding. E-waste creates a different capital/regulatory process; grocery retail adds physical rollout and shrinkage. There is no verified installed-capacity utilization series or commissioned project return cohort that supports a capital-light extrapolation.\\n\\n| Years | ΔNOPAT | Δminimum IC | Same-window proxy | Lagged ΔIC | Lagged proxy | Organic scoreable |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| 3 | 8.2566 | 65.5758 | 12.59% | 37.2930 | 22.14% | No |\\n| 5 | 9.8062 | 67.8158 | 14.46% | NS | NS | No |\\n\\nNumerator is analytical operating NOPAT at a constant 25.17% tax rate, not reported PAT. The minimum operating-capital denominator is receivables + inventory + net PPE − trade payables. Financing capital is equity + debt − total reported cash; residuals expose omitted advances, taxes and accruals rather than forcing reconciliation. Both closing and average ratios are shown later. Three- and five-year incremental and lagged-capital diagnostics do **not** establish organic incremental ROIC: original/restated balance sheets, group formation, unaudited Dubai profits and stock-to-PPE conversion contaminate them. An original FY20 capital endpoint does exist: minimum operating IC is₹10.4104243 crore. There is no comparable RHP-restated FY20 endpoint, so that original balance is not spliced into the five-year lagged series and the lagged return remainsNS. [E03,E11,E17.]\\n\\nFY26 reported ROCE of 15.87% uses EBIT ₹13.7548 crore divided by closing capital ₹86.6740 crore, despite an average-capital caption. It includes other income and must not be silently called independently verified operating ROIC. The model consequently funds inventory, receivables, advances, maintenance and growth capex explicitly. Terminal reinvestment is at least g×NOPAT/15% assumed incremental ROIC, with a 10% sensitivity, as well as the structural working-capital/capex requirement. “Small size” and a large market are not substitutes for those economics.\\n\\n\"","\"Management, Governance & Execution\"","\"\\n**Audits read, proportionate conclusions:** original FY20 and FY21 opinions are unmodified, with CARO reporting and IFC exemption. Original FY22 and FY23 opinions are unmodified, but CARO/IFC are not applicable under the stated small-company basis; they are not affirmative clean internal-controls audits. FY23's later public-company conversion does not retroactively change that scope. Original FY20/21 annexures contain stale boilerplate dates. FY24 audit/annexures, FY25 standalone and consolidated audit/IFC and FY26 equivalents were examined. AKAR & Associates was reappointed for the term beginning September 2024 through 2029. [E17; audit_evidence.json; S02–S09.]\\n\\nFY25's EOM specifically flags ₹8.58 crore inventory capitalization. Statutory arrears and external guarantee disclosures deserve attention, without being relabeled an adverse opinion. FY26 Dubai assets ₹4.5027 crore, revenue ₹5.4987 crore, PAT ₹4.0059 crore and net cash flows ₹1.9610 crore come from management-supplied unaudited information. The resulting 72.85% reported Dubai PAT margin and about 39.54% share of group PAT demand a component audit and a transaction/tax reconciliation, not an allegation. The July segment-response statement that no individual geography met quantitative criteria needs reconciliation with that material result; segment profit and PAT are not assumed identical. [E02,E03,E14; AR26 pp127–128,135–136; S47.]\\n\\nRPTs are material even where current sales are small. FY26 related purchases were ₹1.4046 crore; KMP remuneration ₹0.5729 crore. RDPL receivable ₹9.103 crore versus ₹8.3868 crore exceeds related FY26 sales by a wide margin. CARO identifies ₹8 crore guarantee for Sustainquest and ₹4.95 crore for external RDPL. The former supports consolidated exposure and must not simply be added again to group debt; the latter is a separate contingent stress. A consolidated note with nil component rows but total ₹4.97 crore does not override the ₹12.95 crore gross CARO disclosure. Obtain a counterparty-level roll-forward. [E06,E08; AR26 pp77,109,162–166.]\\n\\nPromoter capital changed with rights dilution. The FY26 capital note shows approximately 50.95% from Aman/Kulbir holdings, versus ~65.11% a year earlier. A declaration dated 7 April 2026 and exchange-filed 17 June says no encumbrance was created during FY26, but its annexure uses a different historical/full-paid-looking share denominator. That is not a verified current pledge-free float calculation. Full-class beneficial ownership and current encumbrance need reconciliation; no blanket “zero pledge” assertion is made. [S48; AR26 pp143–144.]\\n\\n**Dated promise versus outcome audit:**\\n\\n| Statement | Outcome by cutoff | Assessment |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| May 2024: Dubai operations Q2/Q3FY25, meaningful FY26; integration to reduce RPT | Dubai incorporated December 2024 and reports FY26 activity; component unaudited; RDPL debtor remains large | Partial operational delivery, no verified cash-quality resolution |\\n| November 2024: Digi2L JV, 20+ brands, 100 franchise stores in two years, an ambiguous EBITDA-floor response followed by an explicit PAT10% confidence claim | JV terminated March2025; no verified store cohort; franchise target’s two-year deadline is November2026, still future at cutoff | JV not delivered; do not falsely mark a not-yet-due store promise failed |\\n| December2024: ₹100cr rollout within12months, ₹400–500cr FY27 revenue, first e-waste phase in H1FY26 | Proposed ₹535 preferential equity/warrants withdrawn March2025; e-waste cumulative use only₹0.25cr by March2026 | Original financing/commissioning not demonstrated; FY27 target requires ~234–317% annual growth from FY26 |\\n| July2026: AED770,000 loan to Dubai at9% for one year | Approval disclosure says outstanding nil at approval; later disbursement not verified | Not cash already transferred; eliminate internally in consolidated model |\\n| October16: first grocery mart to open October20, investment₹0.50cr | Announcement located, independent commissioning proof not located at cutoff | Planned, not assumed delivered |\\n\\nSources: official calls S21–S22; expansion S28; cancellations S29,S35; monitor S27; loan S36; retail S01. The November2024 call also describes collections within roughly one month and no bad debts; FY26 ageing and RDPL balances require a bridge to that claim. CFO resignation and later MD/CFO signing, corrected machine-readable results and other filing errors are execution/transparency flags, not proof of dishonesty. Governance score4 is a cautious supported judgment; G1 has not been forced to fail.\\n\\n\"","\"Financial Forensics\"","\"\\n| Metric | FY24 | FY25 | FY26 |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| operating_ebitda | 7.5185 | 10.3990 | 16.2899 |\\n| operating_ebit | 7.2526 | 7.8528 | 13.3516 |\\n| normalized_pat_proxy | 4.9878 | 5.2565 | 9.8272 |\\n| minimum_operating_ic | 32.0306 | 45.7693 | 76.7076 |\\n| financing_ic | 33.3458 | 47.2091 | 81.6149 |\\n| closing_roic_proxy | 0.1694 | 0.1284 | 0.1302 |\\n| average_ic_roic_proxy | 0.2515 | 0.1511 | 0.1631 |\\n| reported_fcf_proxy | -17.1248 | -7.0018 | -24.5500 |\\n| inventory | 22.9137 | 25.6845 | 50.0258 |\\n| ar | 5.8831 | 9.2796 | 19.7100 |\\n| ap | 2.2316 | 2.1115 | 6.1254 |\\n| debt | 0.0833 | 7.4883 | 15.3607 |\\n| cash | 1.7936 | 0.6730 | 5.0590 |\\n| end_dso | 43.3269 | 61.0994 | 60.0331 |\\n| end_inventory_days_operating_cost_proxy | 198.9287 | 208.1628 | 176.3403 |\\n| interest_cover | 23.7013 | 14.4672 | 12.4421 |\\n\\nOperating EBITDA removes all other income; operating EBIT excludes it as well. “Normalized PAT” in this table is only reported PAT less other income after analytical tax, not repaired economic earnings. Dubai uncertainty and inventory valuation cannot be cured with a made-up exceptional adjustment. Ratio rows are dimensionless; days are labelled. FY24–26 reported FCF proxies use reported cash-flow capex and therefore carry the noncash reconciliation warnings below. [E02–E07,E11; S02–S04,S41.]\\n\\n| Period / comparability | CFO sum | PAT sum | Ratio of sums |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| FY22_26_mixed_vintage_diagnostic | -32.7914 | 22.3634 | -146.63% |\\n| FY24_26_mixed_scope_diagnostic | -33.7724 | 20.6763 | -163.34% |\\n| FY25_26_consolidated | -18.4343 | 15.4686 | -119.17% |\\n\\nCFO/PAT is a ratio of **sums**, never an average of annual ratios. Mixed-vintage/mixed-scope windows are labelled diagnostics. FY25 CFO was positive, but FY24 and FY26 absorbed substantially more operating cash than profit generated. Capex is below CFO and does not explain a negative operating cash flow.\\n\\n**Cash bridge and capital classification:** FY26 annual-report rounded lakh bridge is PBT1,268 + depreciation294 + interest107 − interest income32 = pre-WC1,637. Inventory−2,434, receivables−1,043, long advances+37, other current assets−633, payables+401, other liabilities+125 and provisions+43 lead to reported cash generated−1,866, tax−277 and CFO−2,143. Rounded lines contain a ₹4 lakh reconciliation residual, retained explicitly. June24 results instead report CFO−₹21.4693 crore and PAT₹10.1334 crore versus annual-report PAT₹10.1309 crore. These are different disclosure vintages, not two independently confirming exact numbers. [S02 p139; S41.]\\n\\nFY25 reported fixed-asset cashflow of ₹9.9975 crore coexists with ₹8.58 crore stock-to-fixed-assets capitalization. Subtracting the latter yields ₹1.4175 crore cashcapex and ₹1.5782 crore FCF **only as a diagnostic reconciliation**, not an established corrected statement. The EOM alone does not establish whether all capitalized goods were opening stock or were bought and deployed during the year. An inventory roll-forward and operating-versus-investing cash classification bridge are required; no mechanical corrected CFO is asserted. FY26 land addition of ₹3.1111 crore includes assumed RIICO debt of ₹2.0124406 crore payable over three years, while cashflow shows about₹3.12 crore PPE purchase. Do not both treat that whole amount as cash spent and add inherited debt as a second purchase; the historical mismatch remains open. Forecast cashcapex and amortization are separately modelled, rather than extrapolating the unreconciled presentation. [E03,E11; S02 pp145,159; S03 pp73–74.]\\n\\n**Working-capital and funding risks:** inventory is roughly₹50 crore, with no SKU-level age, NRV cushion, warranty/return cohort or liquidation recovery history. Receivables include₹8.38 crore older than one year; management describes them as good. Supplier advances are₹6.50 crore versus₹1.23 crore. Overdue related debtor balances can create apparent demand and absorb funds without cash profit. Inventory days use operating cost as a proxy, not a claimed audited COGS-cycle disclosure. [E06,E20; S02 pp151–153.]\\n\\nReported cash₹5.059 crore includes cash-in-hand₹2.57 crore and short deposits₹1.58 crore. The monitoring report says unspent designated e-waste money₹3.99 crore is in an ICICI FD₹4.30 crore maturing7April2027. That FD exceeds the annual-report deposit line and cannot be added again. The model tests explicit usablecash allocations of₹0,₹1.069 and₹3.479 crore; none is asserted as the actual October balance. The₹3.479 usablecash branch pairs with only₹1.58 restricted openingcash, not₹3.99, and ring-fences₹7.23 of the future call; total opening cash never exceeds₹5.059. Monitor table rows total₹21.12 crore raised but one total line shows₹45.27 crore. The original offer proposed₹45.272 crore gross and₹0.80 crore fees, while actual allotment implies₹42.24 crore gross across both instalments. Monitor costs₹0.37 crore differ from annual cashflow issue expenses~₹0.33 crore. Residual fee and proportional use-of-proceeds reconciliation are unresolved, so future calls are not assumed entirely net free cash. A “no deviation” monitoring conclusion is not an audit or a usable-cash reconciliation. [E07,E10; S27.]\\n\\nShort borrowings include callable facilities and current maturities. Margin requirements and <=90-day eligible book debts make reported unused sanctions different from available cash. The note-level tax provision₹2.45 crore, approximate statutory liabilities₹0.39 crore, customer advances₹2.33 crore and other accruals do not perfectly match face totals; the model conservatively pays legacy obligations without automatically monetizing tax assets₹2.94 crore. Existing RIICO/vehicle debt is counted once; current gratuity is settled in the bridge and long legacy gratuity₹0.25 crore before terminal equity. External RDPL guarantee₹4.95 crore is stress-tested separately. [E08–E09; S02 pp145–150.]\\n\\n\"","\"Valuation & Market Expectations\"","\"\\n| Metric | Value |\\n|---|---|\\n| Fullpaid staleclose / lasttrade | 226.35 / 219.00 |\\n| Partlypaid staleclose | 77.85 |\\n| Fullpaidclass marketcap₹cr | 128.0915 |\\n| Partlypaidclass marketcap₹cr | 20.5524 |\\n| Observedbothclasses equity₹cr | 148.6439 |\\n| Fullpaidparity syntheticcurrentequity₹cr | 166.7279 |\\n| Futurecalls₹cr | 21.12, not currentcash |\\n| Observedclasses / FY26reportedPAT | 14.67 |\\n| MechanicalotherincomeadjustedP/E | 15.13 |\\n| FullyfundedclaimP/Ediagnostic | 18.54 |\\n| StaleMarch EV/operatingEBITDA | 9.76–10.00 |\\n| CurrentEV / reliable normalizedP/E | NS / NS |\\n\\nBoth classes last appeared on16October. ROCKPP ₹77.85 plus an eventual₹80 call is₹157.85, well below the full-paid₹226.35; timing, rights, liquidity and settlement differences prevent an arbitrage conclusion. Full-paid price multiplied by all8.299m issued shares is a **fully-funded claim equivalent**, not current equity market capitalization. The class-summed quoted equity value is a different observation. Reported FY26 EPS₹16.56 uses a weighted denominator of6,118,288 shares; dividing PAT by all8,299,000 fully-called claims gives only about₹12.21 before any return on future call cash. Neither denominator should be silently substituted into the other valuation. The model for a full-paid shareholder uses all existing claims, a dated hypothetical call receipt and **zero new shares from that call**. Future call cash is not added to current EV. [E01,E10,E15; S44 and market_extract.json.]\\n\\nEV diagnostics pair the observed class values with stale March debt/cash. Removing only other income does not solve unaudited profit or stock valuation; reliable normalized P/E and current EV remain NS. No FY27 quarterly cash flow or overlapping TTM is invented.\\n\\n**Execution audit:** full-paid records exist for53/60 expected sessions and17/20 recent sessions. Missing rows are ND, not known zero-trade days. Observed60-session mean/median traded value is approximately₹15.8564/₹9.7314 lakh; median volume4,750 shares. Full-paid lot250 costs₹56,587.50 at the stale close; the ₹5 lakh reference rounds up to9lots/2,250shares/₹509,287.50. Depth, spreads, delivery turnover, circuit availability and future buyer interest are not verified. [E15,E19; NSE source hashes and rows in market_extract.json.]\\n\\n| Participation | Whole lots/day | Sessions for9lots | Meaning |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| 2.00% | 0 | Not feasible | No whole lot feasible at this conservative observed participation |\\n| 5.00% | 0 | Not feasible | No whole lot feasible at this conservative observed participation |\\n| 10.00% | 1 | 9 | Observedonlylower-bound diagnostic, not execution guarantee |\\n\\n**Cash-linked scenarios, not predictions:** audited March31 stocks are carried through seven dated bridge intervals to October19, then60 monthly intervals. Eight intraperiod collection/payment cycles distinguish supplier funding before collection; four/sixteen-cycle sensitivities test discretization. Every payment can trigger bounded funding before the payment, never after a prior breach. Bank financing is uncommitted, cumulative gross-capped at₹2 crore; rescue equity is capped at₹15 crore gross and authorized share headroom, issued in **whole shares**, at25% discount with3% fee. Equity rescue cannot invent pre-entry shares contrary to the current master. Interest, principal, legacy tax/statutory/accrual payments, already-issued rights, project cash and dilution are cash-linked. Post-breach continuation is explicitly an unfunded counterfactual, never an ordinary equity return.\\n\\nBase assumptions are20% sales growth,11% operating EBITDA,65 DSO/175 inventory days/22 payable days. Bear:5%/7.5%/95/220/15 with markdowns and70% rollover. Bull:30%/13%/50/140/30, with lower markdowns. These are analyst assumptions, not guidance. Future rights call₹21.12 crore is assumed collected7April2027 only in the main conditional path, less₹0.43 crore assumed residual issue fees and₹4.82 crore ring-fenced for the remaining e-waste project; zero/half/late calls are tested. Existing designated₹3.99 crore and newly ring-fenced call cash fund only e-waste capex after the assumed maturity date, reducing the remaining₹8.81 crore project requirement; it never becomes free operating cash. Grocery capex₹0.50 crore is explicit. The UAE loan is eliminated within the group, not charged twice.\\n\\n| Bridge / case / finance | Survives | First failure | Bank₹cr | Equity₹cr | Terminal₹ | 5yIRR |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| earmarked_allocation/bear/no_optional_finance | False | 2026-07-03 operating_cash_cost | 0.00 | 0.00 | NS | NS |\\n| earmarked_allocation/bear/bounded_optional | False | 2026-11-19 debt_principal_assumed | 2.00 | 15.00 | NS | NS |\\n| earmarked_allocation/base/no_optional_finance | False | 2026-07-03 operating_cash_cost | 0.00 | 0.00 | NS | NS |\\n| earmarked_allocation/base/bounded_optional | False | 2030-04-11 operating_cash_cost | 2.00 | 15.00 | NS | NS |\\n| earmarked_allocation/bull/no_optional_finance | False | 2026-07-03 operating_cash_cost | 0.00 | 0.00 | NS | NS |\\n| earmarked_allocation/bull/bounded_optional | False | 2031-10-19 terminal_all_debt_settlement | 2.00 | 15.00 | NS | NS |\\n| cash_unavailable/bear/no_optional_finance | False | 2026-03-31 assumed_opening_usable_cash_from_audited_total | 0.00 | 0.00 | NS | NS |\\n| cash_unavailable/bear/bounded_optional | False | 2026-03-31 assumed_opening_usable_cash_from_audited_total | 0.00 | 0.00 | NS | NS |\\n| cash_unavailable/base/no_optional_finance | False | 2026-03-31 assumed_opening_usable_cash_from_audited_total | 0.00 | 0.00 | NS | NS |\\n| cash_unavailable/base/bounded_optional | False | 2026-03-31 assumed_opening_usable_cash_from_audited_total | 0.00 | 0.00 | NS | NS |\\n| cash_unavailable/bull/no_optional_finance | False | 2026-03-31 assumed_opening_usable_cash_from_audited_total | 0.00 | 0.00 | NS | NS |\\n| cash_unavailable/bull/bounded_optional | False | 2026-03-31 assumed_opening_usable_cash_from_audited_total | 0.00 | 0.00 | NS | NS |\\n| deposit_only_restricted/bear/no_optional_finance | False | 2026-10-22 operating_cash_cost | 0.00 | 0.00 | NS | NS |\\n| deposit_only_restricted/bear/bounded_optional | False | 2026-11-19 debt_principal_assumed | 2.00 | 15.00 | NS | NS |\\n| deposit_only_restricted/base/no_optional_finance | False | 2026-10-30 operating_cash_cost | 0.00 | 0.00 | NS | NS |\\n| deposit_only_restricted/base/bounded_optional | False | 2030-04-11 operating_cash_cost | 2.00 | 15.00 | NS | NS |\\n| deposit_only_restricted/bull/no_optional_finance | False | 2031-04-22 operating_cash_cost | 0.00 | 0.00 | NS | NS |\\n| deposit_only_restricted/bull/bounded_optional | False | 2031-10-19 terminal_all_debt_settlement | 2.00 | 15.00 | NS | NS |\\n\\nFirst failures before entry indicate that **the chosen underidentified bridge cannot fund itself**, not that a listed company actually defaulted. A path without a historical cash breach may still fail terminal debt/gratuity/reserve conditions. Failure retains the entire chronology but suppresses ordinary terminal price and IRR. No probability-weighted recommendation is constructed from unsupported weights.\\n\\nTerminal operating value is continuing FCFF/(discount−growth), not an arbitrary P/E. Next-year maintenance/growth investment, working capital, minimum economic reinvestment and permanent cash growth are funded. All debt and legacy gratuity are paid before equity; a full continuation year tests peak cash drawdown. Only cash above the retained reserve is distributable. Debt is not subtracted twice; restricted cash remaining is not automatically added; no dividend is counted both in cash and return. Entry1% and exit2% transaction/slippage haircuts are analytical assumptions, not executable cost estimates. A path must also collect the full existing rights call before ordinary equal-share terminal valuation is allowed.\\n\\n| Test | Survives | First failure | Equity₹cr | Terminal₹ | IRR |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| margin_15 | True | None | 14.71 | 207.06 | -2.36% |\\n| margin_20 | True | None | 2.45 | 349.45 | 8.42% |\\n| margin_25 | True | None | 1.65 | 490.04 | 16.00% |\\n| growth_10 | True | None | 3.81 | 94.66 | -16.51% |\\n| growth_30 | False | 2028-08-22 operating_cash_cost | 15.00 | NS | NS |\\n| dso_45 | False | 2031-10-19 terminal_all_debt_settlement | 15.00 | NS | NS |\\n| dso_90 | False | 2028-11-19 operating_cash_cost | 15.00 | NS | NS |\\n| inventory_120 | True | None | 11.65 | 148.16 | -8.68% |\\n| inventory_220 | False | 2028-03-22 operating_cash_cost | 15.00 | NS | NS |\\n| bank_cap_zero | False | 2026-07-03 operating_cash_cost | 0.00 | NS | NS |\\n| rollover_80pct | False | 2030-01-26 operating_cash_cost | 15.00 | NS | NS |\\n| rights_zero | False | 2028-04-15 operating_cash_cost | 15.00 | NS | NS |\\n| rights_half | False | 2029-02-19 earmarked_project_capex_from_free_cash | 15.00 | NS | NS |\\n| rights_late | False | 2030-03-22 operating_cash_cost | 15.00 | NS | NS |\\n| call_fee_80lakh | False | 2030-03-26 operating_cash_cost | 15.00 | NS | NS |\\n| call_ringfence_zero | False | 2030-04-11 operating_cash_cost | 15.00 | NS | NS |\\n| trading_costs_5pct | True | None | 2.45 | 349.45 | 6.91% |\\n| early_collections_50pct | False | 2030-03-12 operating_cash_cost | 15.00 | NS | NS |\\n| cycles_4 | False | 2030-03-26 operating_cash_cost | 15.00 | NS | NS |\\n| cycles_16 | False | 2030-04-17 operating_cash_cost | 15.00 | NS | NS |\\n| guarantee_4_95 | False | 2029-09-26 operating_cash_cost | 15.00 | NS | NS |\\n| inventory_markdown_20 | False | 2029-06-03 operating_cash_cost | 15.00 | NS | NS |\\n| related_ar_markdown_50 | False | 2027-03-01 operating_cash_cost | 15.00 | NS | NS |\\n| terminal_k_13 | True | None | 2.45 | 442.04 | 13.64% |\\n| terminal_k_19 | True | None | 2.45 | 291.58 | 4.56% |\\n| terminal_g_zero | True | None | 2.45 | 347.01 | 8.27% |\\n| terminal_roic_10 | True | None | 2.45 | 310.66 | 5.90% |\\n\\n| Variable | Target | Threshold | Status |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| margin | price_only | 15.88% | Bracketed diagnostic threshold, not a forecast |\\n| margin | hurdle12 | 22.20% | Bracketed diagnostic threshold, not a forecast |\\n| margin | hurdle15 | 24.26% | Bracketed diagnostic threshold, not a forecast |\\n| margin | hurdle18 | 26.54% | Bracketed diagnostic threshold, not a forecast |\\n| margin | 5x | 48.79% | Bracketed diagnostic threshold, not a forecast |\\n| margin | 10x | NS | No crossing found within disclosed range; no extrapolated root |\\n| growth | price_only | 10.19% | Bracketed diagnostic threshold, not a forecast |\\n| growth | hurdle12 | NS | No crossing found within disclosed range; no extrapolated root |\\n| growth | hurdle15 | NS | No crossing found within disclosed range; no extrapolated root |\\n| growth | hurdle18 | NS | No crossing found within disclosed range; no extrapolated root |\\n| growth | 5x | NS | No crossing found within disclosed range; no extrapolated root |\\n| growth | 10x | NS | No crossing found within disclosed range; no extrapolated root |\\n\\nReverse grids test all disclosed points before bracketing crossings; failed or below-target trials are retained. Margin roots hold base working-capital assumptions; growth roots use a separately labelled cash-disciplined16% margin/50DSO/140inventory-day/30payable-day branch. This is not a hidden optimistic base case. A root may be a funding-feasibility boundary rather than a smooth valuation equality. “No crossing” means no solution in the displayed domain, not proof that a solution cannot exist elsewhere. Required high EBITDA margins must be compared with FY26 gross margin of about23.81% and operating EBITDA of about13.59%, not with the anomalous Dubai PAT margin.\\n\\n| Conditional case | 12%hurdle | 15%hurdle | 18%hurdle |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| margin_15 | 114.00 | 99.89 | 87.82 |\\n| margin_20 | 192.40 | 168.58 | 148.21 |\\n| margin_25 | 269.80 | 236.40 | 207.84 |\\n\\nHurdle-entry prices are conditional on the displayed economic and financing policy, not buy levels. They keep the rescue issue-price policy anchored to the observed full-paid price; reducing an investor's purchase price does not magically improve the company's financing terms. All cash, financing, sensitivity and reverse outputs come from the same canonical model. Retention: **189 requests, 189 unique complete paths, 48 failed unique paths**.\\n\\n\"","\"Bear Case & Permanent-Loss Path\"","\"\\nThe strongest bear case is a feedback loop: stock ages or needs discounting, related/old debtors do not pay, eligible collateral falls, banks reduce drawing power, and operating cash becomes insufficient before sales can be collected. Calling rights or raising discounted equity takes time and may not be fully subscribed. External guarantee invocation adds another cash claim. If refinancing only delays an inventory realization loss, book equity does not protect per-share value. A stale SME quotation can prevent a timely exit even before a formal insolvency event. [E06–E10,E19–E20.]\\n\\nIllustratively, a20% haircut on₹50.03 crore inventory is about₹10 crore; a50% loss on the₹9.103 crore RDPL debtor is about₹4.55 crore, before the₹4.95 crore guarantee, taxes and liquidation costs. These are distinct stress channels, not a blanket claim that all exposures fail simultaneously or that guarantees are already debt. No unsupported asset-recovery price floor is inferred from the₹71.31 crore reported equity. The model additionally tests broad receivable haircuts, which are intentionally harsher than a debtor-specific loss. Bear paths without financing do not acquire a finite ordinary return merely because a terminal multiple can be calculated.\\n\\nThesis-break variable: **cash collection and saleable inventory realization relative to cash claims**, rather than reported revenue or EBITDA alone. An independent audited component and a clean cash/guarantee roll-forward can improve the assessment; a further acceleration in aged debtors, stock and funding reliance would worsen it.\\n\\n\"","\"5× / 10× Feasibility\"","\"\\n**Classification: Speculative / unsupported.**\\n\\nFivefold and tenfold five-year outcomes require approximately37.97% and58.49% annualized wealth growth, respectively, before recognizing the assumed trading-cost drag. The reverse tests explicitly target those net wealth multiples and retain failed roots. A higher headline revenue CAGR alone does not achieve them: working capital, tax, project investment, dilution and terminal reinvestment consume the incremental economics.\\n\\nAt a 20% volume/value growth assumption, pushing EBITDA to margins well above current gross-margin economics is not a credible base route to such outcomes. Growth-root tests deliberately improve cash discipline but still pay claims and fund continuation. A “no crossing within range” result is more useful than manufacturing a10× target with an unearned exit multiple. A ten-year 5×/10× return illustration would require further evidence on durable economics; no ten-year operating forecast is invented. The group is small enough to grow substantially, but size is optionality, not proof of high-quality compounding.\\n\\n\"","\"Monitoring Plan\"","\"\\n| Trigger / evidence needed | What would change the assessment | Review timing |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Dubai independent audit and revenue/profit/tax/receivable reconciliation | Verify the material component, its unusual margin and cash remittance ability | Before G2 can clear; at next annual/interim filing |\\n| Bank/FD roll-forward reconciling₹5.059 cash,₹1.58 deposits,₹4.30 FD and₹3.99 designated balance | Establish unrestricted cash, funding location and project money without double counting | Before underwriting current liquidity |\\n| RDPL debtor ageing, settlement receipts and guarantee confirmations | Demonstrate cash recovery; reconcile external₹4.95 and subsidiary₹8 guarantees versus note total | Next results and material RPT updates |\\n| SKU ageing, NRV/write-off cohorts and stock-to-PPE documentation | Explain₹8.58 capitalization, depreciation, actual cashcapex and saleability | Before CAP/EARN scoreability improves |\\n| Call notice, subscription/cash receipt and security conversion | Replace hypothetical call timing/collection with verified events | At each rights announcement |\\n| Eligible borrowing base, facility renewal, covenants and legacy-dues settlement | Distinguish nominal sanctions from drawable cash | At each funding result/renewal |\\n| E-waste commissioning, permits, throughput and project returns | Evidence for a new revenue/capital cohort rather than a narrative | At commissioning and first two operating periods |\\n| Store openings, closures, same-store sales and cash contribution | Test November2026 store ambition and new grocery economics | At original deadline, not prematurely |\\n| Full primary tape, circuits, live depth and actual lot-capacity | Reassess G4 at the same₹5lakh reference and2/5/10% participation | Before any transaction decision |\\n\\nA price decline alone changes prospective IAS inputs, not BQS/GQS. If data resolve, recalculate the original canonical factors, document each change and preserve the prior vintage. No silent score upgrade or ranking insertion is permitted.\\n\\n\"","\"Final Classification & Conditional Action\"","\"\\n**Evidence Hold; research incomplete on material reliability, current funding and tradability.** BQS/GQS/IAS remain NS with transparent bounds. The v3 classification matrix cannot create a favorable category from an incomplete composite, and compounder criteria are not met. This is not an “Avoid on proven fraud” judgment and not a solvency declaration. It is a refusal to underwrite what is not yet supported.\\n\\nConditional action is to resolve the evidence in Section13 and rerun the same model, not trade on the cheapest apparent P/E. Even if reported growth persists, funded cash conversion and intrinsic value **per fully diluted, fully-called share** must improve. No position size or portfolio role is recommended. In a multi-company SME page, retain the identical framework structure, show the Hold conspicuously, and exclude this incomplete issuer from numeric investability ranks rather than assigning an artificial neutral score.\\n\\n\"","\"Sources, Assumptions & Open Questions\"","\"\\nEvidence IDs E01–E20 are in evidence_ledger.json with type V verified disclosure, C calculation, M management statement or A analyst assumption, source locator, confidence and limitation. Primary source byte hashes identify the inspected documents; they do not certify the truth of disclosures. Public source links follow. Original PDFs, OCR, screenshots, service helpers and private upload identifiers are intentionally excluded from the reconstructable research bundles. Concise authored facts and executable calculations remain.\\n\\n| ID | Primary document |\\n|---|---|\\n| S01 | [Capacity addition 16 Oct - Rockingdeals will launch its first Retail Grocery Mart at Sarai on 20 October 2026, with ₹50 lakh investment.](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/rockingdeals_16102026134731_Retail_Expansion_RDCL.pdf) |\\n| S02 | [Annual Report 2026](https://archives.nseindia.com/annual_reports/SME_AR_31795_ROCKINGDCE_2025_2026_A_9082837_07092026205811.pdf) |\\n| S03 | [Annual Report 2025](https://archives.nseindia.com/annual_reports/SME_AR_29118_ROCKINGDCE_2024_2025_A_12716460_17092025171647.pdf) |\\n| S04 | [Annual Report 2024](https://archives.nseindia.com/annual_reports/SME_AR_26314_ROCKINGDCE_2023_2024_A_05102024070034.pdf) |\\n| S05 | [Un-audited-Financial-Results-for-Q1-ending-30.06.2025](https://rdcel.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/Un-audited-Financial-Results-for-Q1-ending-30.06.2025.pdf) |\\n| S06 | [Annual Report FY 2019-2020](https://rdcel.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/Annual-Report-2019-20.pdf) |\\n| S07 | [Annual Report FY 2020-2021](https://rdcel.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/Annual-Report-20-21.pdf) |\\n| S08 | [Annual Report FY 2021-2022](https://rdcel.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/Annual-Report-21-22.pdf) |\\n| S09 | [Annual Report FY 2022-2023](https://rdcel.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/Annual-Report-22-23-RDCEL.pdf) |\\n| S10 | [Annual Report FY 2024-25](https://rdcel.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/Annual-Report-FY-2024-25.pdf) |\\n| S11 | [Annual report 2025-26](https://rdcel.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Annual-report-Rockingdeals-Final-V4.pdf) |\\n| S12 | [Intimation to Stock Exchange regarding MOU signed between Etlus Mode Private Limited and Company](https://rdcel.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/Stock-Exchange-Intimation-regarding-MOU-between-Eltus-and-RDCEL-1.pdf) |\\n| S13 | [Trade Agreement with PUMA](https://rdcel.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/Trade-AGreement-with-PUMA.pdf) |\\n| S14 | [MOU signed between Oriana and The Company](https://rdcel.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/IntimationtostockexchangeregardingMOUsignedbetweenOrianaandRDCEL.pdf) |\\n| S15 | [Intimation regarding joint venture agreement signed with Digi2L](https://rdcel.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/SE-Intimation-joint-venture-agreement-signed-Digi2L-and-RDCEL.pdf) |\\n| S16 | [Press Release-Incorporation of a Subsidiary Company](https://rdcel.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/Pressreleasesubsidiary.pdf) |\\n| S17 | [Intimation regarding resignation of Tarun goel](https://rdcel.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/Intimation-regarding-resignation-of-Tarun-goel.pdf) |\\n| S18 | [Intimation regarding resignation of CFO-Mr. Jitender Verma](https://rdcel.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/Intimation-regarding-resignation-of-CFO-Mr.-Jitender-Verma.pdf) |\\n| S19 | [STatement of Deviation for the Half Year ended on Sep 2024](https://rdcel.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/STatement-of-Deviation-for-the-Half-Year-ended-on-Sep-2024.pdf) |\\n| S20 | [STatement of Deviation for the Half Year ended on March 2025](https://rdcel.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/STatement-of-Deviation-for-the-Half-Year-ended-on-March-2025.pdf) |\\n| S21 | [H2 FY24 Earning Call Transcript](https://rdcel.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/Rockingdeals-Circular-Economy-Limited-Conference-call-Transcript.pdf) |\\n| S22 | [H1 FY25 Earning Call Transcript](https://rdcel.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/Transcript-.pdf) |\\n| S23 | [Investor presentation for H2 and FY 23-24](https://rdcel.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/IntimationtostockechangeregardingInvestorpresentationforH2FY.pdf) |\\n| S24 | [Investor Presentation for H1 FY 24-25](https://rdcel.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/InvestorPresentationforH1FY24-25.pdf) |\\n| S25 | [Letter of Offer Dated: October 13, 2025](https://rdcel.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/Letter-of-Offer.pdf) |\\n| S26 | [RHP](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/emerge/corporates/content/RockingDeals_RHP.pdf) |\\n| S27 | [monitor_Mar26](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/rockingdeals_15052026202555_intimation_of_Submission_of_monitoring_agency__1_.pdf) |\\n| S28 | [expansion_Dec24](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/rockingdeals_30122024105338_businessupdaterdcel.pdf) |\\n| S29 | [withdraw_Mar25](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/rockingdeals_21032025110320_intimationrdcel.pdf) |\\n| S30 | [peer_newjaisa_FY26](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/NEWJAISA2020_27052026195417_OutcomeBM_Financialsresults.pdf) |\\n| S31 | [peer_liquidity_FY25](https://investors.liquidityservices.com/static-files/5bce319c-3b64-46e4-a77e-5560a376ab64) |\\n| S32 | [rockingdeals_10112025165856_outcomeofbmeeting](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/rockingdeals_10112025165856_outcomeofbmeeting.pdf) |\\n| S33 | [rockingdeals_11102025154341_OutcomeofBMdated11102025](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/rockingdeals_11102025154341_OutcomeofBMdated11102025.pdf) |\\n| S34 | [rockingdeals_13012025143507_businessupdate](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/rockingdeals_13012025143507_businessupdate.pdf) |\\n| S35 | [rockingdeals_13032025113858_intimationmou](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/rockingdeals_13032025113858_intimationmou.pdf) |\\n| S36 | [rockingdeals_13072026190944_Intimation_under_regulation_30](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/rockingdeals_13072026190944_Intimation_under_regulation_30.pdf) |\\n| S37 | [rockingdeals_15092026154531_Intimation_to_Stock_Exchange-final](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/rockingdeals_15092026154531_Intimation_to_Stock_Exchange-final.pdf) |\\n| S38 | [rockingdeals_16022026160123_Intimation_for_Strategic_collab__1_](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/rockingdeals_16022026160123_Intimation_for_Strategic_collab__1_.pdf) |\\n| S39 | [rockingdeals_22082025164108_Intimationofloan](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/rockingdeals_22082025164108_Intimationofloan.pdf) |\\n| S40 | [rockingdeals_22082025164426_conversionofunsecuredloan](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/rockingdeals_22082025164426_conversionofunsecuredloan.pdf) |\\n| S41 | [rockingdeals_24062026155237_Update_RDCEL_FS](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/rockingdeals_24062026155237_Update_RDCEL_FS.pdf) |\\n| S42 | [rockingdeals_25112025204640_Outcome_BM_Allotment](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/rockingdeals_25112025204640_Outcome_BM_Allotment.pdf) |\\n| S43 | [rockingdeals_27052026175459_Outcome_of_the_BM](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/rockingdeals_27052026175459_Outcome_of_the_BM.pdf) |\\n| S44 | [rockingdeals_28112025135734_Intimation_Rockingdeals_Revised](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/rockingdeals_28112025135734_Intimation_Rockingdeals_Revised.pdf) |\\n| S45 | [rockingdeals_29032025160327_intimationofwos](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/rockingdeals_29032025160327_intimationofwos.pdf) |\\n| S46 | [rockingdeals_30052025163719_Outcomeofbmrdcel](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/rockingdeals_30052025163719_Outcomeofbmrdcel.pdf) |\\n| S47 | [rockingdeals_31072026115219_Reply_to_NSE__1_](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/rockingdeals_31072026115219_Reply_to_NSE__1_.pdf) |\\n| S48 | [team_sandeshc_17062026125140_21](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/team_sandeshc_17062026125140_21.pdf) |\\n| S49 | [GNG Electronics FY26 primary earnings release, 5 May 2026](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/GNGNSE_05052026194354_Press_Release_FY26_Financial_Results.pdf) |\\n\\nMaterial unresolved questions are the Dubai audit/segment-profit bridge, original/restated history, stock capitalization and cashcapex, available cash/FD reconciliation, guarantee roll-forward, old debtor collection, tax/other-current-liability note mismatches, current borrowing eligibility and complete market executability. A GNG annual-report BSE request returned403; its official earnings release supplies the limited peer comparison. The NSE announcements API returned an empty result despite known filings; it was not treated as evidence of no disclosures. Issuer and exchange-linked filings supplied the audit history and later updates.\\n\\n**Reproducibility:** report, score audit, market audit, financial metrics, all scenario/reverse/sensitivity results and full path archive regenerate offline from the authored inputs and code in either compact or full package. Reconstruction deletes every generated output and attestation first, checks authored hashes against a separately retained baseline, rebuilds with standard-library Python, independently verifies invariants and compares all regenerated hashes. Neither bundle needs the other, raw PDFs or service access. Current owned Library download/readback proofs are held separately, not self-certified inside these files.\\n\\n**All16 final checks, with unresolved evidence visible:**\\n\\n| # | Check | Status | Disposition |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| 1 | Identity / security / dates | PASS | Exactissuer, twoISINclasses, fullpaidquote16Oct vs cutoff19Oct, groupandhistoricalscope explicit. |\\n| 2 | Primary records and attribution | PASS | Originalaudits,annexures,notes,rights,monitoring,executionandpeersread; M/A distinctfromV. |\\n| 3 | Audit / RPT / financing / dilution | PASS with G2/G3 HOLD | Noadverseopinioninvented; materialreliabilityandfundinggapsretained; rightsalreadyissued. |\\n| 4 | Periods and units | PASS | RHP sourcehundreds convertedtocrore; nooverlappingTTM; noFY27quarterlyCFinvented. |\\n| 5 | Revenue decomposition | UNRESOLVED / NS | Nominalgrowthandgroup/standalonebridgecomputed; noverifiedprice-volume-acquisitionorganicseries. |\\n| 6 | Cash conversion | PASS with caveat | CFO/PATratioofsums; cashcapexnoncashreclassificationdoesnotexcuseoperatingcashdeficit. |\\n| 7 | Incremental return | UNRESOLVED / NS | 3y/5ysameandlaggedproxiesexplicit; cohortandorganiccapitalunavailable. |\\n| 8 | Normalization / taxes / claims | PASS with G2 HOLD | Otherincomeremovedmechanically, notfraudadjusted; currentclaimsandfuturecallsseparate; allgroupdebt/taxcounted. |\\n| 9 | Scenarios reconcile | PASS | Datedpreentry/monthly/intraperiodfunding, boundedwhole-sharerescue, rights andrestrictedcash co